WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:27.840
<v Ira Glass>Well, this week, in these troubled and divisive political times, we have this not very typical example of majority rule and what a mess it can make. Our story takes place in a suburban school district an hour north of New York City, East Ramapo, New York. Picture yards, strip-mall sprawl, box stores. Super diverse. You've got big Latino and Haitian and African American populations, some white enclaves, working-class and poor and well-off areas.
<v Ira Glass>在本周，在这个充满困扰和政治分歧的时代，我们看到了一个多数人统治的典型反面教材，以及它可能造成的混乱。我们的故事发生在一个距离纽约市以北一小时车程的郊区学区——纽约州东拉马波。想象一下这里的景象：庭院、带状购物中心、大型零售店。这里非常多元。既有大量的拉丁裔、海地裔和非裔美国人，也有一些白人聚居区，既有工薪阶层和贫困地区，也有富裕地区。

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:55.990
<v Ira Glass>And for a long time now, Hasids have been moving in in large numbers. Hasids are ultra-religious Jews. You've seen the ones in the long black coats and the hats. The men have beards. The women keep their heads covered in public. Like the Amish, they prefer to keep to themselves, so they don't send their kids to public schools. They send them to religious schools called yeshivas, where Yiddish is spoken as the primary language. And in East Ramapo, mostly the Hasids are poor. They're lower middle class.
<v Ira Glass>长期以来，哈西德派犹太人大量迁入。哈西德派是极端宗教化的犹太人。你可能见过那些穿着黑色长外套、戴着帽子的人。男人留着胡须，女人们在公共场合会遮住头发。像阿米什人一样，他们喜欢独处，所以不把孩子送到公立学校，而是送到被称为“叶史瓦”的宗教学校，那里以意第绪语为主要教学语言。在东拉马波，大多数哈西德派教徒比较贫困，属于中下阶层。

00:00:55.990 --> 00:01:10.940
<v Ira Glass>And here was their situation. Because they're living in the suburbs, they're paying high property taxes, which, of course, are high because they're paying for local public schools, which their kids don't go to. And then they're also paying for these private schools, these yeshivas. So they're getting squeezed, right?
<v Ira Glass>这就是他们面临的处境。因为他们住在郊区，所以要缴纳高额的房产税。当然，税收很高是因为他们在为当地的公立学校买单，而他们的孩子并不上这些学校。同时，他们还要负担这些私立学校，即“叶史瓦”的费用。所以他们处境艰难，对吧？

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:36.160
<v Ira Glass>And as the Hasidic population got bigger and bigger, that became an issue. Because in New York, these suburban school district budgets go up for a public vote. As the Hasids numbers increase, there were enough of them that they could conceivably go to the polls and vote down the school budget, force cuts. Though, kind of remarkably, that did not happen. And it didn't happen because the public schools and the Hasids worked out a deal, a truce.
<v Ira Glass>随着哈西德派人口越来越多，这成了个问题。因为在纽约，郊区学区的预算需要公众投票表决。随着哈西德派人数增加，他们的人数足以在投票时否决学校预算，迫使学校削减开支。然而，令人惊讶的是，这种情况并没有发生。没发生的原因是公立学校和哈西德派达成了一项协议，一种休战。

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.800
<v Ira Glass>Steve White is an activist in school board politics in town.
<v Ira Glass>史蒂夫·怀特是镇上校董会政治活动家。

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:54.270
<v Steve White>The original deal that was made many, many years ago was, if we don't investigate whether or not there's education going on in the yeshivas, then the rabbis won't tell their people to vote down our school budget.
<v Steve White>多年前达成的最初协议是，如果我们不去调查“叶史瓦”学校是否在进行真正的教育，拉比们就不会告诉信徒去投票否决我们的学校预算。

00:01:54.270 --> 00:02:19.110
<v Ira Glass>In other words, the school board won't call in the state to check and see if math and reading and history are being properly taught in the yeshivas, like the state mandates, if the Hasids would just stay away from the polls. Jason Friedman was superintendent for East Ramapo schools for eight years. He says the truce is actually even cruder than that. He says that the yeshivas are entitled to money from public school budgets for certain things like buses and books.
<v Ira Glass>换句话说，如果哈西德派远离投票站，校董会就不会请州政府去核查“叶史瓦”学校是否按州政府要求教授数学、阅读和历史。杰森·弗里德曼曾担任东拉马波学校主管长达八年。他说，这种休战协议实际上比这更粗糙。他说，“叶史瓦”学校有权从公立学校预算中获得资金，用于校车和课本等项目。

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:31.940
<v Ira Glass>And he says the deal was to give the Jewish schools as much money as was legal. Friedman says he would regularly meet with Hasidic rabbis and yeshiva administrators. His pitch was always, we will give you whatever we can. Just don't vote down our budgets.
<v Ira Glass>他说，协议的内容是尽可能合法地为这些犹太学校提供资金。弗里德曼说他经常会见哈西德派拉比和“叶史瓦”的管理人员。他的话术总是：我们会尽力满足你们，只要你们不去投票否决我们的预算。

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:49.690
<v Jason Friedman>I think that's what we all worked at. We tried to work out a quid pro quo situation. We could probably never ask a non-public school family to come out and vote yes on a budget. Our hope was that they wouldn't come out, that they would stay away.
<v Jason Friedman>我想这就是我们所有人努力的目标。我们试图达成一种“以物易物”的局面。我们可能永远无法要求非公立学校的家庭出来投票支持预算。我们的希望是他们不出来投票，保持远离。

00:02:49.690 --> 00:03:07.750
<v Ira  Glass>So the Hasidic population grew. And here's a curious demographic fact about East Ramapo. Most of the people in East Ramapo were not Hasids, but most of the children were. Two out of three children in the school district were Hasidic.
<v Ira  Glass>哈西德派人口不断增长。关于东拉马波，有一个奇怪的人口统计事实。东拉马波的大多数居民不是哈西德派，但大多数孩子是。学区里三分之二的孩子是哈西德派。

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:25.860
<v Ira  Glass>And the Hasids felt cheated because property taxes kept going up to pay for these schools that most of the kids in the district did not attend. And also, the monies and services that the public schools were supposed to be giving the Hasidic schools, they didn't feel like it was enough. So the detente ended.
<v Ira  Glass>哈西德派感到受到了欺骗，因为房产税不断上涨，用来资助那些区内大多数孩子并不就读的学校。此外，他们认为公立学校本应提供给哈西德学校的资金和服务也不够。于是，缓和期结束了。

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:41.090
<v Ira  Glass>Our story this week is about what happened once it fell apart. And I should say, if you think that you have heard your share of stories about school board fights, what happened next, I totally assure you was like nothing you have probably seen in any school district anywhere. Because it raised such basic questions.
<v Ira  Glass>本周我们的故事就是关于这一切破裂后发生的事情。我得说，如果你认为你已经听够了校董会争斗的故事，那我完全向你保证，接下来的事是你可能在任何其他学区都没见过的。因为它提出了如此基本的问题。

00:03:41.090 --> 00:04:02.970
<v Ira  Glass>It's usually, you know, just a given in our country that people who don't drive still pay taxes for roads, and people who hate the outdoors pay for public parks, and people without kids in the public schools pay for the public schools. Just that's the deal. We all know it. And so what happens when people who do not want to pay for the public schools take over the school board? We're devoting our whole hour to this today.
<v Ira  Glass>在我国，人们通常认为，即使不开车的人也要为道路缴税，讨厌户外活动的人也要为公园买单，没有孩子上公立学校的人也要为公立学校出钱。这就是契约，我们都知道。那么，当那些不想为公立学校买单的人掌控了校董会时，会发生什么呢？今天我们整整一小时都在讨论这个问题。

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:21.190
<v Ira  Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. If, by the way, you're listening to this by podcast or internet, there is some cursing in this show, and we have unbeeped the curses in this internet version of the episode. If you prefer a beeped episode, you can get that at our website.
<v Ira  Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《这就是美国生活》节目，我是艾拉·格拉斯。顺便提一下，如果你是通过播客或网络收听，本节目中含有一些咒骂内容，我们在这个网络版本中保留了这些词汇。如果你更喜欢有消音的版本，可以在我们的网站上获取。

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:29.450
<v Ira  Glass>Ben Calhoun spent months visiting East Ramapo over the course of 2013 and 2014. This is a rerun of our show from a few years ago. He tells the story.
<v Ira  Glass>本·卡尔霍恩在2013年和2014年期间多次访问东拉马波。这是几年前我们节目的重播。由他来讲述这个故事。

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:49.570
<v Ben Calhoun>The dispute that finally shattered the truth in East Ramapo-- there's no way around this-- it's kind of regulate-y and fussy. But this is what ended the peace, what sent the East Ramapo school district into a political brawl that's lasted years. The dispute had to do with special ed students-- Hasidic special ed students.
<v Ben Calhoun>最终导致东拉马波休战协议破裂的纠纷——对此避无可避——涉及一些行政规范和繁文缛节。但这正是结束和平、使东拉马波学区陷入长达数年政治斗争的原因。这场纠纷与特殊教育学生有关——哈西德派的特教学生。

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:54.460
<v Yossi Gestetner>It was fully in the hands of the district to be a mensch and to do things the right way. They simply refused.
<v Yossi Gestetner>学区完全有能力表现出善意并以正确的方式行事。他们只是拒绝了。

00:04:54.460 --> 00:05:19.410
<v Ben Calhoun>Yossi Gestetner is a political operative, an activist, in the Hasidic communities. He said the problem had to do with the fact that Hasidic special ed students, like other special ed kids, they need expensive therapies and services and education. And the government will pay for those, is required to pay for them. But for that to happen, the district would usually require that the kids go into a public school setting.
<v Ben Calhoun>约西·格斯特特纳是哈西德社区的一名政治操作员和活动家。他说，问题在于哈西德派的特教学生和其他特教孩子一样，需要昂贵的治疗、服务和教育。政府会为这些买单，也必须买单。但通常情况下，学区要求孩子们进入公立学校环境才能获得这些服务。

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:44.010
<v Ben Calhoun>The Hasidic and the ultra-Orthodox live in closed communities. And the private yeshivas they send their children to are all Hasidic kids. So just picture-- you're a Hasidic parent with a special needs kid-- maybe with developmental and emotional issues-- and people are telling you, you want to get your kid the help they need, you gotta yank them out of the environment where they're the most comfortable-- the only environment they've ever known-- and send them elsewhere.
<v Ben Calhoun>哈西德派和极端正统派生活在封闭的社区中。他们送孩子去的私立“叶史瓦”学校全是哈西德派的孩子。所以请想象一下——你是一名哈西德派家长，孩子有特殊需求——也许有发育或情绪方面的问题——而人们告诉你，想让孩子获得所需的帮助，你必须把他们从最舒适、唯一熟悉的环境中拉出来，送到别的地方去。

00:05:44.010 --> 00:06:21.100
<v Yossi Gestetner>The parents just want to make sure that the children go to an environment and in a school, an institution, where the language spoken is the language they understand best, the tradition, the religion, and the culture. The way they see it is the state has an obligation to provide the education, and some special needs students get up to $27,000 in funding. Take this $27,000, put it into a different institution, an institution which works for the parent and for the child, a place where it helps the student and the parents best-- especially if it doesn't cost an extra nickel for the taxpayers.
<v Yossi Gestetner>家长们只是想确保孩子们身处一个他们最能理解的语言、传统、宗教和文化的环境中。在他们看来，州政府有义务提供教育，而一些有特殊需求的学生能获得高达27,000美元的资助。拿这27,000美元投入到另一个对家长和孩子都有利的机构中去，这不仅能帮助学生，而且如果这不会让纳税人额外多花一分钱，何乐而不为呢？

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:27.730
<v Ben Calhoun>In other words, you're going to spend the money anyways. What do you care if it goes to a yeshiva?
<v Ben Calhoun>换句话说，反正这笔钱是要花的。如果它流向“叶史瓦”学校，你又有什么可在意的呢？

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:55.440
<v Ben Calhoun>But it's not that simple. You can't do that because of federal education regulations. The government says if it's going to pay for special ed students, then they have to be placed in mainstream environments as much as possible. Congress made that the law because, for years, our country had a real problem-- that special ed kids were being warehoused and isolated. And according to the law, public schools are just considered to be more mainstream than private religious schools.
<v Ben Calhoun>但事情没那么简单。你不能那样做，因为有联邦教育法规。政府规定，如果要为特教学生支付费用，他们必须尽可能被安置在主流教育环境中。国会之所以立法，是因为多年来，我们的国家确实存在特教孩子被“仓储”和隔离的严重问题。根据法律，公立学校比私立宗教学校更具主流化。

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:11.990
<v Ben Calhoun>So in East Ramapo, Hasidic parents would often go to the school district and they'd ask for their special ed children to be put in private yeshivas using government money. The school district would say, sorry, we have to follow the law, and the law says no-- which I brought up with Yossi Gestetner.
<v Ben Calhoun>因此在东拉马波，哈西德派家长经常要求学区用政府资金将他们的特教孩子安置在私立“叶史瓦”学校。学区会说，抱歉，我们必须遵守法律，而法律不允许——这一点我也和约西·格斯特特纳讨论过。

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:14.670
<v Ben Calhoun>You know, the law just has a very clear--
<v Ben Calhoun>你知道，法律非常明确地……

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:16.120
<v Yossi Gestetner>Yeah, but I don't--
<v Yossi Gestetner>是的，但我并不……

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:19.670
<v Ben Calhoun>--and distinct preference for keeping them in a public setting.
<v Ben Calhoun>……并且明确倾向于将他们留在公共场所。

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:41.810
<v Yossi Gestetner>First of all, if the law tortures the children, the law should be changed. So don't throw around "the law." I mean, law was used to agitate against people all the time. So if the law is broken, don't force the district to spend a million dollars to fight this damn law. Change the law and finish. It's never an argument to me-- like, well, it's against the law. So change the damn law.
<v Yossi Gestetner>首先，如果法律在折磨孩子，法律就应该修改。所以别拿“法律”说事。法律一直被用来煽动对立。如果法律有问题，别强迫学区花一百万美元去和这该死的法律斗争。修改法律就行了。对我来说，这从来都不是理由——比如，“好吧，这违反法律”。那就修改该死的法律。

00:07:41.810 --> 00:08:05.120
<v Ben   Calhoun>These complaints and frustrations grew and grew year after year. And so did the Hasidic and ultra-Orthodox population. Eventually, that population was at a point where it wasn't big enough to do what Gestetner is saying-- change the damn law-- but it was big enough to do something else.
<v Ben   Calhoun>这些抱怨和挫折年复一年地增长。哈西德派和极端正统派的人口也在增长。最终，该群体的人数不仅达到了格斯特特纳所说的“修改法律”的要求，而且足以做别的事了。

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:13.100
<v Mimi Calhoun>When it started to matter to the Orthodox, I guess, that they would win-- I want to show you some of these numbers.
<v Mimi Calhoun>我想当他们意识到能赢的时候，这对正统派来说就开始变得重要了——我想给你看看这些数字。

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:33.950
<v Ben Calhoun>This is Mimi Calhoun. She's a former East Ramapo school parent elected to the school board in 2004. She showed me the school board voting results from the last few decades, how things changed around 2007, 2008, when the Hasidim made a move for control of the school system by taking over the school board.
<v Ben Calhoun>这是米米·卡尔霍恩。她是一位前东拉马波学校家长，于2004年当选校董。她向我展示了过去几十年的校董会投票结果，显示了2007年、2008年左右局势是如何变化的，当时哈西德派通过控制校董会，试图掌控学校系统。

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:48.310
<v Ben Calhoun>I want to just say, when you hear these totals, the numbers are going to maybe sound small. Remember, these are suburban school board elections. People winning with a few thousand or even a few hundred votes-- that's the norm in any district around here.
<v Ben Calhoun>我想说的是，当你听到这些总数时，这些数字听起来可能很小。请记住，这是郊区校董会选举。赢得选举只需几千甚至几百票——这在这一带的任何学区都是常态。

00:08:48.310 --> 00:09:02.370
<v Mimi Calhoun>I had about 3,000 votes. My opponent had about 2,000. And those were sort of the numbers that we had. A total of maybe 5,000 people voted. The next year, when Aron Wieder ran--
<v Mimi Calhoun>我当时有大约3000票。我的对手有大约2000票。这就是我们当时的数字。总共可能有5000人投票。第二年，当阿伦·维德参选时……

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:06.690
<v Ben Calhoun>Aron Wieder was one of the first Hasidic candidates elected to the board.
<v Ben Calhoun>阿伦·维德是首批当选校董的哈西德派候选人之一。

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:27.510
<v Mimi Calhoun>--he had 6,000 votes, double what I had. That was astonishing. Our numbers stayed about the same, 2,000 or 3,000, and the Orthodox votes tripled. And then their numbers just started increasing.
<v Mimi Calhoun>……他获得了6000票，是我票数的两倍。这太惊人了。我们的票数保持在大约2000或3000，而正统派的票数翻了三倍。然后他们的数字就开始不断增加。

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:46.240
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>It was almost scary the way they-- it's like, no matter what we did-- like, if we could bring out 5,000 votes, they could bring out 7,000 votes. It was almost as if they could calculate how many people we might be able to get to, and they could bring out that many more people.
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>这几乎让人害怕……无论我们怎么做——即使我们能动员5000人投票，他们就能动员7000人。仿佛他们能算出我们能争取到多少人，然后他们就能比我们多动员出那么多人。

00:09:46.240 --> 00:10:17.020
<v Ben Calhoun>This is Suzanne Young-Mercer. She's another former school board member. And like she's saying, it's remarkable to look at these elections, to see how the totals start climbing after 2007. It makes me think of that Bugs Bunny cartoon with Elmer Fudd, where they're racing up in the barber chairs. Vote totals go from 5,000 to 8,000, 13,000 16,000 18,000. You can see both sides turn it up, though the Hasidic-backed candidate's always a little bit out front.
<v Ben Calhoun>这是苏珊·杨-默瑟，另一位前校董会成员。正如她所说，查看这些选举结果非常令人震惊，可以看到2007年之后的总票数是如何攀升的。这让我想起兔八哥和埃尔默·福德那部动画片，他们坐在理发椅上比赛。投票总数从5000涨到8000、13000、16000、18000。你可以看到双方都在发力，尽管哈西德派支持的候选人总是稍稍领先。

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:26.390
<v Ben Calhoun>Lots of people on the public school side talk about this with bitterness and awe. They tell these stories about Hasidic voters dressed in black filing out of buses.
<v Ben Calhoun>公立学校阵营的许多人带着痛苦和敬畏谈论这件事。他们讲述着关于哈西德派选民穿着黑色衣服从巴士上走下来的故事。

00:10:26.390 --> 00:11:06.780
<v Suzanne  Young-mercer>If my husband was here, he'd say, when he was out in front of the schools handing out stuff, he remembers that someone on the religious side got on a telephone and called to say they needed more people out here to vote. And he said, within minutes, he felt like it was like a sea of people just showed up and were voting. Organized political takeover. Absolutely. Absolutely.
<v Suzanne  Young-mercer>如果我丈夫在这里，他会说，当他在学校门口发传单时，他记得宗教阵营那边有人打电话说需要更多人出来投票。他说，几分钟内，他感觉就像一大群人涌了出来进行投票。绝对是有组织的政治接管。绝对是这样。

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:27.680
<v Ben   Calhoun>The takeover was gradual. The East Ramapo school board has nine members, but only three seats go up for election every year. By 2008, the majority was six Hasidic/ultra-Orthodox, three traditional public school folks.
<v Ben   Calhoun>这次接管是渐进的。东拉马波校董会有九名成员，但每年只有三个席位改选。到2008年，多数席位已由哈西德派/极端正统派占据，仅剩三名传统公立学校成员。

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:55.850
<v Ben   Calhoun>Once the Hasidic majority took control, at first many of the ways it started to remake the district were incremental-- changes to personnel and administration here and there. Kind of quickly, though, restricting the budget and reining in property taxes became a preoccupation. The school board determines the property tax levy when it sets its budget. Controlling taxes meant trimming corners off the budget, dropping extracurriculars, sports teams, cutting music teachers in elementary schools.
<v Ben   Calhoun>哈西德派多数派获得控制权后，起初他们重塑学区的方式是渐进的——比如人事和行政方面的零星变动。然而，很快他们就将削减预算和控制房产税视为重中之重。校董会在制定预算时决定房产税税率。控制税收意味着在预算上精打细算，取消课外活动、体育队、削减小学音乐教师。

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:57.750
<v Woman>They heard that six of their music teachers are going to be cut.
<v Woman>他们听说六名音乐教师将被裁减。

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:15.830
<v Ben Calhoun>In response, parents and students started to get involved, showing up at board meetings to criticize and question. Grumbling and grousing spiked in this whole new way when the Hasidic-dominated board predicted declining enrollment for the district. And as a result, it planned to close two schools.
<v Ben Calhoun>作为回应，家长和学生开始参与进来，出现在会议上进行批评和质疑。当哈西德派掌控的校董会预测学区入学率将下降，并因此计划关闭两所学校时，不满和抗议达到了前所未有的程度。

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:17.520
<v Man>Don't close our schools.
<v Man>不要关闭我们的学校。

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:33.020
<v Ben Calhoun>With all the growth in the Hasidic community, people knew there was an intense need for land and buildings for private Jewish schools. So almost immediately, there was speculation the shuttered public buildings would be sold to yeshivas. Gradually, the boardroom took on a new dynamic.
<v Ben Calhoun>随着哈西德社区的增长，大家都知道他们对私立犹太学校的土地和建筑有强烈需求。所以几乎立即有人猜测，关闭的公立建筑会被卖给“叶史瓦”学校。渐渐地，董事会会议室呈现出一种新的态势。

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:36.090
<v Student>(SHOUTING) I am a student here! You need to listen to me!
<v Student>（高喊）我是这里的学生！你们得听我说！

00:12:36.090 --> 00:13:00.090
<v Ben Calhoun>Eventually, on one side of the room, you'd have the public school parents and students. They're mostly black, Latino, some recent immigrants, many poor. More than 70% of students qualify for free and reduced lunch. And then up on the dais, you'd have the Hasidic/Orthodox majority, governing the future of a school system that they'd never send their own kids to.
<v Ben Calhoun>最终，在会议室的一边，是公立学校的家长和学生。他们大多是黑人、拉丁裔，一些是新移民，许多人很贫困。超过70%的学生符合免费或减价午餐条件。而在主席台上，坐着哈西德派/正统派多数派，主宰着一个他们自己的孩子永远不会就读的学校系统的未来。

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:09.210
<v Ben Calhoun>Amid the mounting controversy, the Hasidic majority started this regular routine, saying the board needed to go into executive session-- private session in a back room.
<v Ben Calhoun>在矛盾不断升级的情况下，哈西德派多数派开始了一种固定的程序，称董事会需要进入闭门会议——后室里的私人会议。

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:10.370
<v Woman>No executive!
<v Woman>不要闭门会议！

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:13.520
<v School Board Member>[INAUDIBLE] --sustained. It's 5 to 1. We're going to go to executive session.
<v School Board Member>[听不清] ——维持。5比1。我们要进入闭门会议了。

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:14.270
<v School Board Member>[BOOING AND JEERING]
<v School Board Member>[嘘声和嘲笑声]

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:31.240
<v Ben Calhoun>The board would stay locked away for hours on end. Two hours, three hours. And with the board gone, parents and students who had school and work the next day, they'd be left waiting in the boardroom until 11, 12 o'clock at night, waiting for a chance to speak.
<v Ben Calhoun>董事会可能会在里面锁住几个小时。两个小时，三个小时。董事会离开后，第二天还要上学和工作的家长和学生们只能一直等到晚上11点、12点，等待发言的机会。

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:48.910
<v Ben Calhoun>Steadily, the wounds and the bruises and the contentious issues multiplied. But if there was a real shot across the bow, one move that transformed a nasty feud into an all-out war, it came down to one particular board meeting, one night, and this dramatic personnel change.
<v Ben Calhoun>伤口、淤青和争议事项稳步增加。但如果说有一记真正的警钟，将一场恶劣的争吵转化为全面战争，那就是在一次特定的董事会会议上，在那晚，发生了那次戏剧性的人事变动。

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:51.770
<v Ben Calhoun>[DOOR OPENING]
<v Ben Calhoun>[开门声]

00:13:51.770 --> 00:14:07.070
<v Ben Calhoun>November 18, 2009. This is the board meeting where all this happened. At the meeting, the board did what had become so usual. It called for an executive session and it started walking out. But as that happened, something unusual took place.
<v Ben Calhoun>2009年11月18日。这就是发生这一切的董事会会议。在会议上，董事会做了变得习以为常的事。要求进入闭门会议，并开始离开。但就在那时，发生了不寻常的事情。

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:31.850
<v Ben Calhoun>The remaining, quote unquote, "public school advocates" on the board-- this is the minority-- they stopped on their way out of the room. Generally, they didn't associate with the activists and the parents who'd show up. But tonight, they walked right up to them, and they pleaded. Essentially, what they said was, no matter how long we're behind closed doors, whatever you do tonight, please do not leave.
<v Ben Calhoun>剩下的所谓的“公立学校倡导者”——这是少数派——在走出房间时停了下来。通常情况下，他们不与那些前来抗议的活动家和家长联络。但今晚，他们直接走向他们并恳求道。他们本质上说的是，无论我们在关起门来讨论多久，无论你们今晚做什么，请千万不要离开。

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:34.280
<v School Board Member>Executive session. Going back [? for the ?] meeting.
<v School Board Member>闭门会议。回到会议。

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:54.310
<v Ben Calhoun>The board didn't come back into the room until nearly a quarter to 1:00 in the morning. And when they get back, what gets put on the table is this kind of puzzling proposal to fire the district's law firm of many years and hire a new guy. And I say puzzling because listen to the specifics.
<v Ben Calhoun>直到凌晨近1点，董事会才回到房间。当他们回来时，摆上桌子的是一项令人费解的提案：解雇该学区合作多年的律师事务所，聘请一个新人。之所以说令人费解，是因为听听具体细节。

00:14:54.310 --> 00:15:08.720
<v Ben Calhoun>The old lawyer was a local guy, had gone to school in the district, even. He'd done the system's legal work for years and years. And the administration, by every account that I've heard, they really liked him. He charged $120 an hour.
<v Ben Calhoun>原来的律师是当地人，甚至曾就读于该学区。他多年来一直为系统处理法律事务。据我所知，各方对他的评价都很高。他每小时收费120美元。

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:21.950
<v Ben Calhoun>The new lawyer was a guy named Al D'Agostino. He was from out on Long Island, more than an hour away. And his contract, it would be for $250 an hour, plus $125 an hour for travel.
<v Ben Calhoun>新律师名叫阿尔·达戈斯蒂诺。他来自长岛，距离这里一个多小时车程。他的合同是每小时250美元，外加每小时125美元的差旅费。

00:15:21.950 --> 00:15:41.660
<v Ben Calhoun>Add to that this. The new guy had been caught up in a pension investigation by the state of New York. Not exactly desirable. And a confusing move, right? Especially if money is a concern. But people in the room were not confused when this came up. They knew very well who D'Agostino was.
<v Ben Calhoun>加上这个：这名新人还卷入了纽约州的一项养老金调查中。这可不是什么好名声。这是一个令人困惑的举动，对吧？尤其是当他们声称关心金钱的时候。但房间里的人在提出这个问题时并不困惑。他们很清楚达戈斯蒂诺是谁。

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:54.800
<v Ben Calhoun>D'Agostino had recently been in the news for representing another school district, one in Lawrence, Long Island. D'Agostino had been involved in a controversial plan that that board was trying to push through-- a plan to close a school.
<v Ben Calhoun>达戈斯蒂诺最近因代表另一个长岛劳伦斯学区而上过新闻。达戈斯蒂诺参与了该校董会试图推进的一项有争议的计划——关闭一所学校的计划。

00:15:54.800 --> 00:16:05.260
<v Reporter>The public school board, made up primarily of Orthodox Jewish members who send their kids to private school. A few months ago, the board voted to shut this elementary school.
<v Reporter>公立校董会主要由送孩子去私立学校的正统派犹太成员组成。几个月前，董事会投票决定关闭这所小学。

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:14.320
<v Ben Calhoun>Parents out in Lawrence alleged the plan was to close the school in question and then sell it to a yeshiva. And in fact, it would later be sold to a yeshiva.
<v Ben Calhoun>劳伦斯的家长们指控，计划是关闭该学校，然后将其卖给一家“叶史瓦”学校。事实上，它后来确实被卖给了一家“叶史瓦”学校。

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:24.460
<v Reporter>Attorney Albert D'Agostino says the board has every right to close the school and reassign the kids. And rumors that it will be sold to a Jewish yeshiva are just rumors.
<v Reporter>律师阿尔伯特·达戈斯蒂诺表示，董事会有权关闭学校并重新分配学生。关于它将被卖给犹太“叶史瓦”学校的传言只是传言。

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:31.160
<v Albert D'agostino>Right now, the only decision that's been made is that it will be closed and mothballed.
<v Albert D'agostino>目前，唯一做出的决定是它将被关闭和封存。

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:56.390
<v Ben Calhoun>So prior to this meeting, people in Lawrence, Long Island, had been in touch with the public school crowd in East Ramapo. They said, this lawyer is helping our school board do these controversial things. First, they're shutting the school. And also, he's doing this thing where he's helping them put special education students in private yeshivas using public money. Anyway, we've heard your district might hire him. We wanted to warn you.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以在此次会议之前，长岛劳伦斯的人们就一直与东拉马波的公立学校群体保持联系。他们说，这个律师正在帮助我们的校董会做这些有争议的事情。首先，他们关闭了学校，此外，他还在做一件事，帮助他们用公款将特殊教育学生安置在私立“叶史瓦”学校。总之，我们听说你们学区可能要雇佣他。我们想警告你们。

00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:57.230
<v Woman>We didn't know--
<v Woman>我们不知道……

00:16:57.230 --> 00:16:58.520
<v Woman>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Woman>[交叉声音]

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:00.560
<v Woman>It wasn't on the agenda and no one--
<v Woman>议程上没有写，也没有人……

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:15.830
<v Ben Calhoun>In the video of this meeting from this night in East Ramapo, as soon as the majority proposes changing law firms, things unravel super fast. Before you know it, pretty much everyone other than the Hasidic majority is trying to stop the hiring of the new lawyer.
<v Ben Calhoun>在东拉马波当晚会议的录像中，多数派一旦提议更换律师事务所，事情就迅速失控。不知不觉中，除了哈西德派多数派之外的几乎每个人都在试图阻止聘用新律师。

00:17:15.830 --> 00:17:33.410
<v Ben Calhoun>The superintendent projects that hiring D'Agostino will cost the cash-strapped district as much as $900,000 more per year. Remember, this is already a district that's trimming its budget. The superintendent and the public school advocates on the board, they ask over and over just where is that money going to come from?
<v Ben Calhoun>学校主管预计，聘请达戈斯蒂诺每年将使现金匮乏的学区多花费90万美元。记住，这是一个已经在削减预算的学区。学校主管和董事会中的公立学校支持者不断询问这笔钱从哪儿来？

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:39.780
<v Ben Calhoun>While all that's happening, the half dozen public school parents and activists left in the room, they heckle the board.
<v Ben Calhoun>在这一切发生的同时，留在房间里的那几名公立学校家长和活动家不断地嘲讽董事会。

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:40.370
<v Parent>Crooks!
<v Parent>骗子！

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:43.160
<v Ben Calhoun>Sometimes they just shout the board down.
<v Ben Calhoun>有时他们只是大声打断董事会的话。

00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:43.970
<v Parent>Shame!
<v Parent>无耻！

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:45.930
<v Parent>And you're trying to save the district money?
<v Parent>你们还想为学区省钱？

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:46.710
<v Parent>Shame!
<v Parent>无耻！

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:48.380
<v Parent>How could you even think of that?
<v Parent>你们怎么想得出来的？

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:50.560
<v Parent>God is watching you.
<v Parent>上帝在看着你们。

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:51.840
<v Parent>That is disgusting!
<v Parent>那太恶心了！

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:53.730
<v Parent>God is watching you.
<v Parent>上帝在看着你们。

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:54.970
<v Parent>That is absolutely disgusting.
<v Parent>那绝对恶心。

00:17:54.970 --> 00:18:00.840
<v Parent>And is he going to do the same thing for East Ramapo he's doing for Lawrence, New York?
<v Parent>他会像在纽约劳伦斯做的那样，在东拉马波做同样的事吗？

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:02.220
<v Parent>I can't believe I'm even hearing this.
<v Parent>我简直不敢相信我在听这些。

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:23.320
<v Ben Calhoun>Perhaps no one tries more desperately to put the brakes on this whole thing than the superintendent at the time. He argues and he pleads and he says, we don't have the money. He also says this whole plan is half-baked and sloppy. He points out that there's no strategy to hand off the district's current cases from the old lawyers to the new one.
<v Ben Calhoun>当时的主管可能在竭力阻止这一切。他辩论、恳求，说我们没有这笔钱。他还说整个计划是不成熟且草率的。他指出，根本没有策略将学区现有的案件从旧律师移交给新律师。

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:25.870
<v Former Superintendent>I've heard nothing about a transition plan.
<v Former Superintendent>我没听说有任何过渡计划。

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:33.200
<v Ben Calhoun>He says, in disbelief, the district has cases in court tomorrow. There's no reason to do this so hastily.
<v Ben Calhoun>他难以置信地表示，学区明天就有案件要上法庭。没有理由如此草率地行事。

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:44.520
<v Former Superintendent>You should table this now. You're talking millions of dollars. You can't, as a board, change attorneys overnight. I take that back. You can.
<v Former Superintendent>你们现在应该搁置这个议题。你们谈论的是数百万美元。作为董事会，不能一夜之间更换律师。我收回这句话。你们可以。

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:45.460
<v Man>Mr. [? Wieder, I'm ?] [? sorry-- ?]
<v Man>维德先生，对不起……

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:53.050
<v Former Superintendent>You have the right to do that. It would be insane to do it. You're putting this district in major jeopardy, major jeopardy.
<v Former Superintendent>你们有权这样做。但这样做简直是疯了。你们正在让这个学区陷入重大危险，重大危险。

00:18:53.050 --> 00:19:02.800
<v Ben Calhoun>The superintendent is still asking where all the money will come from when Aron Wieder, the leader of the Hasidic majority, cuts him off, calls for a vote.
<v Ben Calhoun>当哈西德派多数派的领袖阿伦·维德打断他并要求投票时，主管仍在询问这笔钱从哪里来。

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:05.310
<v Former Superintendent>Are you going to answer the question of where the money is?
<v Former Superintendent>你们打算回答这笔钱从哪里来的问题吗？

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:06.000
<v Man>No, he's not.
<v Man>不，他不打算回答。

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:07.520
<v Aron Wieder>All those in favor, say aye.
<v Aron Wieder>赞成的人请说“赞成”。

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:23.930
<v Ben Calhoun>Five members in the Hasidic majority vote yes. The three other board members present vote no. The motion passes, and Al D'Agostino becomes the board's new lawyer.
<v Ben Calhoun>五名哈西德派多数成员投了赞成票。在场的另外三名董事会成员投了反对票。动议通过，阿尔·达戈斯蒂诺成为董事会的新律师。

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:45.760
<v Ben Calhoun>With the new lawyer, Al D'Agostino, at the legal rudder, the board forged ahead with the two school closures. Remember, the reasoning had been that the board was predicting a significant decrease in public school enrollment. Enrollment never actually did decline. But the two schools were shuttered and put on the market, and the board eventually sold both to yeshivas.
<v Ben Calhoun>在新律师阿尔·达戈斯蒂诺的法律掌舵下，董事会强行推进了学校关闭计划。记住，他们的理由是预测公立学校入学人数将大幅下降。入学人数实际上从未下降。但两所学校被封存并投放市场，董事会最终将它们都卖给了“叶史瓦”学校。

00:19:45.760 --> 00:20:01.780
<v Ben Calhoun>It also changed its approach to special ed placements. It replaced the district's head of special education, who Hasidic parents had had a ton of beef with, and then the district started placing special ed students in private Jewish schools in very large numbers.
<v Ben Calhoun>它还改变了特殊教育安置的方式。它撤换了学区的特教主管——该主管与哈西德派家长的关系非常紧张——随后学区开始大规模地将特教学生安置在私立犹太学校。

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:11.740
<v Ben Calhoun>You might wonder, how would they do that if it's against the law? The answer is Al D'Agostino, who imported a strategy he'd used in the other school district.
<v Ben Calhoun>你可能会想，如果这违反法律，他们怎么能这样做？答案是阿尔·达戈斯蒂诺，他从另一个学区引入了这一策略。

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:38.610
<v Ben Calhoun>Now, I won't get into all the gritty details here, but the short of it is that D'Agostino kind of cut a messy loophole into the regulations, one that allowed the board to put special education students into yeshivas using public money. These questionable placements were soon piling up in East Ramapo. And state officials were desperately trying to intervene, warning the district that the practice was against the law.
<v Ben Calhoun>现在，我不会在这里讨论所有棘手的细节，简而言之，达戈斯蒂诺在法规中切开了一个混乱的漏洞，允许董事会用公款将特教学生送入“叶史瓦”学校。这些存在疑问的安置案例很快在东拉马波堆积如山。州政府官员极力试图干预，警告学区这种做法违反法律。

00:20:38.610 --> 00:21:03.850
<v Ben Calhoun>Also high on the priorities list for the new Hasidic majority was reining in property taxes. And with the levers of power now in its possession, it launched what's pretty much been a systematic defunding of the East Ramapo public schools. Just to get a picture of how this played out inside the schools, I went to a high school principal named Jean Fields. She'd just retired from one of the district's two high schools, Ramapo High School.
<v Ben Calhoun>新的哈西德派多数派名单上的另一项首要任务是控制房产税。随着权力的在手，他们发动了基本等同于系统性削减东拉马波公立学校经费的行动。为了了解这种情况在学校内部的体现，我去找了一位名叫简·菲尔兹的高中校长。她刚从该学区的两所高中之一——拉马波高中退休。

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:07.630
<v Jean Fields>The yearbooks-- this is 2007, '08, '09, '10.
<v Jean Fields>这是2007、08、09、10年的年鉴。

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:15.490
<v Ben Calhoun>Fields showed me four yearbooks from when the school board really started cutting. You can actually see the yearbooks get thinner and thinner and thinner.
<v Ben Calhoun>菲尔兹向我展示了校董会开始大幅削减开支时期的四本年鉴。你实际上可以看到年鉴变得越来越薄。

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:19.290
<v Jean Fields>It's like half the size by the time you get to 2010.
<v Jean Fields>到2010年，它几乎只有原来的一半大。

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:23.510
<v Ben Calhoun>And that's not declining enrollment. That's things disappearing from the school.
<v Ben Calhoun>那不是因为入学人数下降，而是因为学校里的东西在消失。

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:24.950
<v Jean Fields>Right. That's things disappearing.
<v Jean Fields>对，东西在消失。

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:31.560
<v Ben Calhoun>Fields went page by page, showing me what was cut. The business department-- that was several pages-- all eliminated.
<v Ben Calhoun>菲尔兹一页一页地展示被削减的内容。商业系——那是好几页的内容——全部被取消。

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:33.010
<v Jean Fields>All of these courses are gone.
<v Jean Fields>这些课程全部没了。

00:21:33.010 --> 00:21:46.910
<v Ben Calhoun>There were other departments cut by 80%. Full pages of honor societies, sports, JROTC, AP classes, clubs, dance, home ec, administrative staff. District-wide, the cuts have been sweeping.
<v Ben Calhoun>其他部门被削减了80%。整页的荣誉社团、体育、初级预备役军官训练营（JROTC）、AP课程、俱乐部、舞蹈、家政、行政人员。在全学区范围内，削减是全面的。

00:21:46.910 --> 00:22:28.990
<v Ben Calhoun>Here's a partial list from the last five years. All deans, all social workers eliminated. All assistant principals at the elementary school level, music instruction for elementary school kids, funding for field trip buses-- all eliminated. That came with cuts to administration, guidance counselors, teaching assistants, maintenance, assistant principals, among lots of others. Kindergarten was reduced from full-day to half-day. System-wide over the last six years, the district's laid off 22% of all its teachers. At one point, Jean Fields says, there got to be so few teachers in her building, there was a conversation about whether the school should just let seniors go halfway through the day.
<v Ben Calhoun>这是一份过去五年的部分名单。所有的院长、所有的社会工作者都被解雇了。小学部的所有助理校长、小学儿童的音乐指导、实地考察巴士的资金——全部被削减。同时削减的还有行政、指导咨询师、助教、维护人员、助理校长等等。幼儿园从全日制缩减为半日制。在过去六年中，全学区裁掉了22%的教师。简·菲尔兹说，有一次她所在学校的教师人数少得可怜，甚至有人讨论学校是否应该让高年级学生中午就提前放学。

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:31.120
<v Ben Calhoun>[DRUM LINE PLAYING]
<v Ben Calhoun>[鼓乐演奏]

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:52.700
<v Ben Calhoun>From what I can tell, nothing and no one was sacred. Most high schools have things that are sources of pride. It might be a sports team or a theatre program, a debate team. For Fields' school, it was their marching band. It won big awards. It was featured in the Denzel Washington remake of The Manchurian Candidate. In 2012, the board laid off the band's director.
<v Ben Calhoun>据我所知，没有什么人或事是神圣的。大多数高中都有值得自豪的东西。可能是运动队、戏剧节目或辩论队。对于菲尔兹的学校来说，那是他们的行进乐队。它赢得了大奖，还被丹泽尔·华盛顿翻拍的《满洲候选人》引用。2012年，董事会裁掉了乐队指挥。

00:22:52.700 --> 00:23:03.490
<v Olivia Castor>We would always complain to the school board about scheduling. And what would happen is the school board would always respond and say, well, that's not true. You guys are making it up.
<v Olivia Castor>我们总是向校董会抱怨排课问题。而校董会总是回应说，那不是真的。你们在编造。

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:22.880
<v Ben Calhoun>Olivia Castor went to Spring Valley, the other of the district's two high schools. She was there during the biggest cutbacks. And while she was there, so many teachers were laid off, students started having a hard time filling their schedules with actual classes, a complaint that Castor took to the board, where she was told the problem wasn't real.
<v Ben Calhoun>奥利维亚·卡斯托就读于该学区另一所高中——斯普林瓦利高中。她在削减最严重的时期在那里就读。当时太多的老师被裁，学生们开始难以排满正式课程。卡斯托向校董会提出了投诉，但被告知问题不是真实的。

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:27.960
<v Olivia Castor>So I was tired of them saying that's not true, so this is a document that we put together about--
<v Olivia Castor>所以我厌倦了他们说那不是真的，所以这是我们整理的一份文件，关于……

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:43.170
<v Ben Calhoun>Castor set out to prove the problem was real. She gathered evidence into this eight-page packet that she showed to me. It includes real student schedules from her high school. These are from 2012, so this is a snapshot from later in the cutbacks.
<v Ben Calhoun>卡斯托决心证明问题的真实性。她收集证据整理成了这份八页的文件，并展示给我看。其中包括她高中时期的学生课表。这是2012年的，是削减后期的一个快照。

00:23:43.170 --> 00:24:02.410
<v Olivia  Castor>And if you look at the schedules, you'll see three things. You'll see study halls, lunches, and community service-- filler periods. Normally, during those periods, you'd have electives or classes or APs that you're taking. For this student, they have like four lunches and two study halls in one day, and you have eight periods in a day.
<v Olivia  Castor>如果你看课表，你会看到三件事：自习课、午餐和社区服务——充数时期。通常在这些时段，你应该有选修课或课程，或参加AP课程。对于这个学生来说，他们一天中有四顿午餐和两节自习，而一天总共只有八节课。

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:05.100
<v Ben Calhoun>And this is grade 12? So this is a senior.
<v Ben Calhoun>这是12年级？所以这是高三学生。

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:09.090
<v Olivia Castor>Yeah, that's a senior. You have three lunches back to back and a study hall.
<v Olivia Castor>是的，这是高三。你有三节连着的午餐和一节自习。

00:24:09.090 --> 00:24:23.810
<v Ben Calhoun>Like the student would have period one, environmental science. Then they would have a study hall. Then they would have lunch. Then they would have lunch. Then they would have lunch. Then they would have public affairs. Then they would have lunch again. And then they would have algebra and trigonometry.
<v Ben Calhoun>就像这名学生第一节是环境科学。然后是自习课。然后是午餐。然后还是午餐。然后还是午餐。然后是公共事务课。然后又是午餐。然后是代数和三角学。

00:24:23.810 --> 00:24:31.220
<v Olivia Castor>Yeah. So basically, three periods out of the eight are learning, and then five out of the eight-- more than half-- is not learning.
<v Olivia Castor>是的。所以基本上八节课中有三节在学习，而五节——超过一半——没有学习。

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:58.840
<v Ben Calhoun>The former principal, Jean Fields, she confirmed this was a problem in her school, too. At one point, she told the administration the problem was so bad some kids might take five years to graduate because they just couldn't take enough required classes. Just to say, the reason this situation could exist-- New York State education law has no regulations about how many hours of instruction a student actually has to get throughout the school day.
<v Ben Calhoun>前校长简·菲尔兹证实她学校里也存在这个问题。有一次，她告诉行政部门问题太严重了，一些孩子可能需要五年才能毕业，因为他们根本无法修够足够的必修课。需要说明的是，这种情况之所以存在，是因为纽约州教育法对学生全天必须接受多少小时的教学没有任何规定。

00:24:58.840 --> 00:25:17.270
<v Ben Calhoun>Olivia Castor eventually took the schedules she'd collected and showed them to the school board. I asked her how they responded. She said, they told me those must be fake.
<v Ben Calhoun>奥利维亚·卡斯托最终带着她收集的课表展示给校董会看。我问她校董会是如何回应的。她说，他们告诉她，那些肯定是假的。

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:47.640
<v Ben Calhoun>As you can imagine, this course of events, this kind of breakdown, this kind of environment, it's produced a sprawling epic of public confrontation. Year after year filled with all the usual kinds of events-- rallies outside Central Office, press conferences, handmade signs, student walkouts, sit-ins. Though even the predictable events often have these details that seem to speak to the peculiarity of this specific war. Like, say, protests where hundreds of students fill a gymnasium and chant things like, "Cut the lawyer."
<v Ben Calhoun>如你所料，这一连串的事件、这种崩溃、这种环境产生了一部宏大的公开对抗史诗。年复一年，充满了各种司空见惯的事件——中央办公室外的集会、新闻发布会、手写标语、学生罢课、静坐示威。甚至那些可预测的事件往往也包含着一些细节，似乎道出了这场特殊战争的特殊性。比如，成百上千名学生挤满体育馆，高呼“裁掉那个律师”之类的口号。

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:49.770
<v Students>Cut the lawyer! Cut the lawyer! Cut the lawyer! Cut the lawyer!
<v Students>裁掉律师！裁掉律师！裁掉律师！裁掉律师！

00:25:49.770 --> 00:26:22.610
<v Ben Calhoun>Another unique part of this big, sprawling fight is that, with the board regularly shutting itself behind closed doors in executive session, in their absence, parents and students would end up holding these strange, impromptu, non-meeting meetings, where they only had each other to talk to. There's a video that I can't quite shake of a meeting like this. It's from December 2012, a meeting about possible budget cuts. Among the parents, there's a mother. Her name is Cassandra Edwards.
<v Ben Calhoun>这场宏大斗争的另一个独特之处在于，董事会经常将自己锁在闭门会议中，在他们不在的时候，家长和学生最终会举行一些奇怪的、临时性的非会议，在那里他们只能彼此交谈。有一个我挥之不去的视频片段，那是2012年12月的一次关于预算削减的会议。在家长中有一位母亲，名叫卡桑德拉·爱德华兹。

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:28.740
<v Cassandra Edwards>Good evening. I think I come from a different perspective. I come from the perspective of being angry at this point.
<v Cassandra Edwards>大家晚上好。我想我来自不同的视角。我来自此刻感到愤怒的视角。

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:49.400
<v Ben Calhoun>As Edwards takes the mic, she stands in front of this auditorium. She's still in her winter coat, facing the crowd. And as she starts to talk, you can see that the board has left the room. They've gone into executive session. All their empty chairs are on the auditorium stage behind her, one for each public official who won't hear anything she's about to say.
<v Ben Calhoun>当爱德华兹拿起麦克风时，她站在礼堂前。她还穿着冬大衣，面对着人群。随着她开始说话，你可以看到董事会已经离开了房间。他们进入了闭门会议。他们所有空荡荡的椅子都在她身后的礼堂舞台上，每一个空座都属于一个即将对她所说的话充耳不闻的公职人员。

00:26:49.400 --> 00:27:08.080
<v Cassandra Edwards>They're not shooting our kids with guns. They are stealing their education. And we are acting as if it is normal. We are acting as if it's OK. We are allowing them to sit in another room and not demand them to come out and speak. Who is the problem? We are the problem! We need to get angry about what's happening with our kids!
<v Cassandra Edwards>他们没有用枪射杀我们的孩子，他们在窃取他们的教育。而我们表现得好像这是正常的。我们表现得好像这没关系。我们允许他们坐在另一个房间里，而不要求他们出来说话。谁才是问题所在？我们就是问题所在！我们需要对孩子们身上发生的事情感到愤怒！

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:08.410
<v Cassandra Edwards>[CHEERING]
<v Cassandra Edwards>[欢呼声]

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:09.680
<v Cassandra Edwards>And we're not angry!
<v Cassandra Edwards>而我们并不愤怒！

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:28.420
<v Ben   Calhoun>I just want to pause here because you'll notice the crowd, they're totally with her. But as she keeps talking, she gets more and more upset, and it turns into this kind of upset that it's hard to cheer for.
<v Ben   Calhoun>我想在这里暂停一下，因为你会注意到人群完全支持她。但随着她继续说下去，她变得越来越不安，那种不安让人难以再欢呼。

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:41.840
<v Cassandra   Edwards>We're losing our kids! And nobody's angry! Nobody's mad! There's not options for our kids. No options. They're setting up prisons for our kids.
<v Cassandra   Edwards>我们要失去我们的孩子了！没有人愤怒！没有人疯狂！我们的孩子没有选择。没有选择。他们在为我们的孩子建立监狱。

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:51.830
<v Jay   Grossman>I'm wondering, is there nothing good about this district and its work? Wouldn't it be nice and productive if you would compliment the board once in a while?
<v Jay   Grossman>我在想，这个学区及其工作就没有一点好的地方吗？如果你偶尔能称赞一下董事会，难道不是既美好又有建设性的吗？

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:52.810
<v Audience>No. No.
<v Audience>不。不。

00:27:52.810 --> 00:27:54.390
<v Jay Grossman>Is it really against your nature?
<v Jay Grossman>这真的违背了你们的本性吗？

00:27:54.390 --> 00:28:07.390
<v Ben Calhoun>Less often, but occasionally, folks from the Hasidic and ultra-Orthodox communities would show up at board meetings, and they'd return fire. In this video, a man named Jay Grossman lays into the public school parents and students.
<v Ben Calhoun>虽然少见，但偶尔会有来自哈西德派和极端正统派社区的人出现在董事会会议上进行还击。在这段视频中，一个名叫杰伊·格罗斯曼的人严厉抨击了公立学校的家长和学生。

00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:33.600
<v Jay Grossman>When you utilize this microphone to spew hatred and negativity constantly, you have accomplished one thing. You have accomplished that I, my friends, family, and acquaintances became more involved in what's going on here. And trust me, our presence here in this room is growing and will grow from week to week. When you constantly attack one of ours, we will be here to defend. We will be here to counterattack if needed. Rest assured--
<v Jay Grossman>当你利用这个麦克风不断散布仇恨和消极情绪时，你只达成了一件事。你让我的朋友、家人和熟人更多地参与到这里发生的事情中。相信我，我们在房间里的存在感正在增长，并且会每周都在增长。当你不断攻击我们中的一个人时，我们会在这里进行防卫。如果需要的话，我们会在这里反击。请放心……

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:35.530
<v Woman>He's threatening us.
<v Woman>他在威胁我们。

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:38.840
<v Jay  Grossman>--Mr. Wieder is going nowhere, and we're going nowhere.
<v Jay  Grossman>……维德先生哪儿也不会去，我们也哪儿也不会去。

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:54.880
<v Ben Calhoun>Gradually, shouting, open hostility, profanity, that became the norm on the school board. And in terms of how deep the political chasm got, there's a couple artifacts I'll include for you. The first is just an anecdote.
<v Ben Calhoun>渐渐地，大喊大叫、公开敌意和粗言秽语成为了校董会的常态。关于政治鸿沟的深度，我有几个样本可以提供给你。第一个仅仅是一个轶事。

00:28:54.880 --> 00:29:29.350
<v Ben Calhoun>At one point, people close to this situation who thought this breach could be repaired, they tried to broker peace talks, a sort of diplomacy summit. A lawyer that specializes in negotiations was brought in. He was a former sergeant in the Israeli military, and folks thought he could make inroads with both sides. Someone close to the negotiation says everybody sat down and the Hasidic leadership was blunt. In about these words, a Hasidic board member said, we have all the power. Why would we negotiate? If we do, we only have things to lose.
<v Ben Calhoun>曾经一度，那些认为这种裂痕可以修复的人试图促成和平谈判，一种类似于外交峰会的活动。有人请来了一位专门从事谈判的律师。他曾是以色列军队的中士，人们以为他能在这双方之间取得进展。一位接近谈判的人说大家都坐了下来，但哈西德派领导层直言不讳。大意是，一位哈西德派董事会成员说，我们拥有所有的权力。为什么要谈判？如果我们这样做，我们只有失去的东西。

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:46.010
<v Ben Calhoun>There's also a candid document, one that points to the political will behind the board and what it was doing. It's this letter written around the time when all this was beginning. A prominent real estate developer in the Hasidic community, a man named Yehuda Weissmandl, wrote a letter to the local newspaper.
<v Ben Calhoun>还有一份坦率的文件，指向了董事会背后的政治意志及其所作所为。这是在这一切开始时写的一封信。哈西德社区的一位著名房地产开发商，名叫耶胡达·魏斯曼德尔，给当地报纸写了一封信。

00:29:46.010 --> 00:30:01.330
<v Ben Calhoun>"Dear fellow taxpayer in the East Ramapo school district, again and again, I read about how upset you are about the members of the school board, how we bloc-voted them in, how we don't have the interests of the schoolchildren at heart. Well, let's take a closer look at that.
<v Ben Calhoun>“致东拉马波学区的同胞纳税人，我一次又一次地读到你们对校董会成员有多么不满，我们是如何以集团投票方式让他们当选的，我们是如何不把在校学生的利益放在心上的。好吧，让我们仔细看看。”

00:30:01.330 --> 00:30:13.630
<v Ben Calhoun>For many years, you took our tax money-- year after year, increase after increase-- and you never had any problem with that. But when we finally get together and say that's enough, that is a problem.
<v Ben Calhoun>“多年来，你们拿走了我们的税款——年复一年，增长后再增长——你们对此从未有过任何问题。但当我们终于团结起来说受够了的时候，这就成了问题。”

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:26.870
<v Ben Calhoun>I have a solution. How about giving all of us the option to bow out of the public school system and keep our money in our pockets? You want our money and our silence. Sorry, you cannot have it all your way."
<v Ben Calhoun>“我有一个解决方案。让我们所有人都拥有退出公立学校系统的选择权，并把我们的钱留在自己的口袋里，怎么样？你们想要我们的钱和我们的沉默。抱歉，你们不能事事都如愿以偿。”

00:30:26.870 --> 00:30:34.830
<v Ben Calhoun>The author of that letter, Yehuda Weissmandl, is now the president of the East Ramapo school board.
<v Ben Calhoun>那封信的作者耶胡达·魏斯曼德尔，现在是东拉马波校董会的主席。

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:48.850
<v Ira   Glass>Ben Calhoun. Coming up, ugliness gets uglier. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira   Glass>本·卡尔霍恩。接下来，丑陋变得更加丑陋。芝加哥公共广播电台节目继续。

00:30:48.850 --> 00:31:22.880
<v Ira Glass>This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. We're hearing today about a strange case of what democracy and what majority rule can look like. It's the story of the East Ramapo, New York, school district, where a group of parents whose kids did not attend public schools took over the public school board for years. In East Ramapo, the non-Hasidic families continued in each election cycle to try to win additional seats on the school board, turn back the Hasidic majority. They never were able to. Ben Calhoun's story from 2014 continues.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《这就是美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们听到了一个关于民主和多数统治看起来会是什么样子的奇怪案例。这是纽约东拉马波学区的故事，一群孩子不上公立学校的家长接管了公立校董会多年。在东拉马波，非哈西德派家庭在每个选举周期继续尝试赢得校董会的额外席位，扭转哈西德派多数派，但他们从未成功。本·卡尔霍恩2014年的故事继续。

00:31:22.880 --> 00:31:47.640
<v Ben Calhoun>I've tried to talk to Hasidic members of the board, current and former. I've made multiple requests in person and on the phone to talk to Aron Wieder, the first leader of the majority. I've also reached out multiple times to the current board president. Neither will return my calls. I've issued a blanket invitation to the whole school board, which is now made up entirely of Hasidic-backed members. I've done that twice via the district clerk with no takers.
<v Ben Calhoun>我尝试与董事会的哈西德派成员交谈，包括现任和前任。我曾多次当面或通过电话要求与第一位多数派领袖阿伦·维德对话。我也曾多次联系现任董事会主席。两人都不回我的电话。我曾两次通过地区职员向整个校董会发出过公开邀请，如今校董会完全由哈西德派支持的成员组成，但无人应答。

00:31:47.640 --> 00:32:21.160
<v Ben Calhoun>The one Hasidic board member who would talk to me is a former board president named Morris Kohn. He sat on the school board from 2009 to 2011, so he was there for some of the most severe cuts. Kohn told me he'd talk to me because he didn't want people to think he'd been, quote, "a madman." This is how Kohn says the story went from the Hasidic and ultra-Orthodox side. He says, flat out, the community wanted control of the board to do all the things we've outlined-- rein in property taxes, change special ed, and to make sure private yeshiva students got all the government money they could for things like busing and books.
<v Ben Calhoun>唯一愿意与我交谈的哈西德派董事会成员是曾任董事会主席的莫里斯·科恩。他在2009年至2011年期间担任董事会成员，所以他在最严重的几次削减期间都在场。科恩告诉我他愿意与我交谈，因为他不希望人们认为他是一个所谓的“疯子”。这就是科恩从哈西德派和极端正统派角度讲述的故事。他说，非常直白地说，社区想要控制董事会以完成我们概述的所有事情——控制房产税、改变特教政策，并确保私立“叶史瓦”学校的学生能获得尽可能多的政府资金，用于校车和课本等项目。

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:32.720
<v Morris Kohn>They were there for that one main purpose, is to make sure the services private students are supposed to get are given, and that the public school was functioning properly.
<v Morris Kohn>他们的目的只有一个，就是确保私立学生应得的服务得到落实，并确保公立学校正常运作。

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:47.370
<v Ben Calhoun>Is there something inherently uncomfortable about a system being governed by folks who don't use that system or wouldn't want to use that system? Is there something that's uncomfortable just about that?
<v Ben Calhoun>被那些不使用该系统或不愿意使用该系统的人所治理，这在本质上是否让人感到不安？仅仅是这一点，就让人感到不安或不公平吗？

00:32:47.370 --> 00:33:17.890
<v Morris Kohn>Well, it's very understandable people would see it that way. It's uncomfortable or unfair. Having said that, every person living in a district, if they're investing money in a business and they feel, OK, the business is not being run properly, then I would get myself in there and try to get those improvements done.
<v Morris Kohn>嗯，人们会这样看是可以理解的。它令人不安或不公平。话虽如此，区内的每个人，如果他们对某项业务进行投资，如果他们觉得这项业务经营不当，那么我也会进去并尝试让那些改进得到落实。

00:33:17.890 --> 00:33:36.780
<v Ben Calhoun>Let me explain the second part of what Kohn had to say because this is less obvious. Kohn says going into all this, the Hasidic community saw the public schools as a system in trouble, bloated with unions and inefficiency, and a good slap of fiscal conservatism would go a long way.
<v Ben Calhoun>让我解释科恩所说的第二部分，因为这不太明显。科恩说，在进入这一切时，哈西德社区认为公立学校是一个陷入困境的系统，充满了工会和低效，而如果进行财政保守主义的冲击，将会有很大的作用。

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:52.500
<v Ben Calhoun>But then, once the Hasidim had control, there were all kinds of external financial factors that came crashing down-- the recession, the state property tax cap. And like school districts all across the state, he says, East Ramapo was suddenly super hard-up.
<v Ben Calhoun>但后来，一旦哈西德派掌握了控制权，各种外部财务因素随之崩溃——经济衰退、州房产税上限。他说，和州内各地的学区一样，东拉马波突然变得非常艰难。

00:33:52.500 --> 00:34:25.520
<v Morris Kohn>State cuts, government funding-- things were getting increasingly difficult to provide even the most simple services. And the board needed to do sometimes draconian cuts, which obviously, parents and students alike did not like that, which is very understandable. And people were feeling, I don't want the school board to do this. I don't want the school board to cut that. Our hands were tied. Like it or not, this is what we need to do.
<v Morris Kohn>州政府削减开支，政府拨款——即使提供最简单的服务也变得越来越困难。董事会不得不进行有时甚至残酷的削减，这显然家长和学生都不喜欢，这是可以理解的。人们感觉，我不希望校董会这样做，我不希望校董会削减那个。我们的手被束缚了。无论喜欢与否，这就是我们需要做的。

00:34:25.520 --> 00:34:34.290
<v Ben Calhoun>Kohn says it wouldn't have mattered who was on the board. Cuts were inevitable. But he says there was so much mistrust, nobody could see this.
<v Ben Calhoun>科恩说无论谁在董事会上都一样。削减是不可避免的。但他表示，因为互不信任，没有人能看清这一点。

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:48.940
<v Morris Kohn>It would always be the private school parents against the public school parents, and I felt in such an environment, no matter what you will do, in one way or another, eventually you will be blamed for it.
<v Morris Kohn>这总是私立学校家长对抗公立学校家长，我觉得在这样的环境中，无论你做什么，最终你都会被责怪。

00:34:48.940 --> 00:34:59.550
<v Ben Calhoun>This argument-- that cuts were inevitable in East Ramapo, that they were making the same tough choices as other districts-- it's not supported by the facts.
<v Ben Calhoun>这种论点——即东拉马波的削减是不可避免的，他们正在做出与其他学区相同的艰难选择——并不被事实支持。

00:34:59.550 --> 00:35:35.460
<v Ben Calhoun>I've analyzed the budgets of all the school districts in the county for the last 10 years. All the other regular school districts in the area, districts that faced the same economy and made it through without the same kinds of massive cuts, the main thing they were doing was raising property taxes. During the five years that East Ramapo was making its biggest cuts, all of the normal neighboring school districts raised their property taxes by an average of more than 25% just to get by. During the same five years in East Ramapo, the Hasidic-controlled board raised taxes by just 9%.
<v Ben Calhoun>我分析了过去10年县里所有学区的预算。该地区所有其他普通学区，面对同样的经济情况并熬过去而没有进行同样大规模削减的学区，他们所做的主要事情是提高房产税。在东拉马波进行最大削减的那五年里，所有邻近的普通学区为了维持生计，平均将房产税提高了25%以上。而在东拉马波的这五年里，由哈西德派控制的董事会仅将税收提高了9%。

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:57.120
<v Ben Calhoun>Some more numbers. During the last 10 years, every comparable school district in the county grew its budget by an average of 50%. East Ramapo's budget grew by 33%. Which, to a layperson, you might say, well, oh, the budget grew. How bad could that be? I actually kind of thought that at first.
<v Ben Calhoun>再看看其他数字。在过去10年里，县里每个相当规模的学区预算平均增长了50%。东拉马波的预算增长了33%。作为一个外行，你可能会说，嗯，预算增长了。那能有多糟？我最初确实也这样想。

00:35:57.120 --> 00:36:21.420
<v Ben Calhoun>But I talked to school administrators and experts who said that the costs the Hasidim and other conservatives say are out of control actually are rising alarmingly fast-- pensions, health care, union contracts, cost of living. Those things grow by so much that a 30% budget increase, that isn't growth. That's devastation.
<v Ben Calhoun>但我与学区管理人员和专家交谈，他们表示哈西德派和其他保守派声称失控的那些成本，实际上正以惊人的速度增长——养老金、医疗保险、工会合同、生活成本。这些东西的增长幅度如此之大，以至于30%的预算增长根本不是增长。那是灾难。

00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:48.570
<v Ben Calhoun>One uniquely volatile element in this chemical reaction, something unexpected that's become a chapter unto itself, is the outsized role of the board's controversial lawyer, Albert D'Agostino. In some ways, it seems like he was just symbolic because of how he was hired. In that way, he became a sort of hood ornament for the Hasidic majority.
<v Ben Calhoun>在这个化学反应中一个极其不稳定的因素，一件意想不到的事情，已经成为一个独立章节的，是董事会有争议的律师阿尔伯特·达戈斯蒂诺的作用。从某些方面来看，他似乎仅仅是象征性的，因为他的聘用方式。就这样，他成为了哈西德派多数派的一种“车头装饰”。

00:36:48.570 --> 00:37:15.340
<v Ben Calhoun>But in other ways, he became an actual central player. He's functioned as an overt advocate for the Hasidic majority in board meetings. He's become a fixture at the end of the board table, right up on the dais. After months of going to meetings, I found he acts like an unusual combination of attorney, legal architect, consigliere, and bully. A lot of the time, if board members shy away from conflict, D'Agostino steps in as a combatant.
<v Ben Calhoun>但在其他方面，他成为了一个实际的核心参与者。他在董事会会议上发挥了哈西德派多数派公开代言人的作用。他成为了董事会桌尽头、主席台上的一名固定成员。在参加了几个月的会议后，我发现他表现得像是一名律师、法律架构师、咨询师和恶霸的奇怪组合。很多时候，如果董事会成员回避冲突，达戈斯蒂诺会以战斗者的身份介入。

00:37:15.340 --> 00:37:22.090
<v Ben Calhoun>A video of one board meeting that gives you a sense of this-- a few months after D'Agostino arrived, a former student came to a meeting.
<v Ben Calhoun>董事会会议的一段视频让你对此有所了解——达戈斯蒂诺到来几个月后，一名前学生来到会议上。

00:37:22.090 --> 00:37:23.840
<v Lorenzo Labitigan>My name is Lorenzo Labitigan.
<v Lorenzo Labitigan>我叫洛伦佐·拉比蒂根。

00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:43.240
<v Ben Calhoun>Lorenzo Labitigan was a district graduate in his first year at Yale, and he'd come back because he'd heard there were possible cuts to advanced placement classes. He wanted to plead to keep those. At some point, he makes a comment about D'Agostino, about how the board's paying all this added money for a lawyer.
<v Ben Calhoun>洛伦佐·拉比蒂根是学区毕业生，当时在耶鲁大学读大一，他回来是因为听说AP课程可能面临削减。他想请求保留这些课程。在某个时候，他评论了达戈斯蒂诺，说董事会支付了所有这些额外费用来雇佣一个律师。

00:37:43.240 --> 00:37:55.810
<v Ben Calhoun>Then he says something about D'Agostino having stained credentials. He's referring to the fact that the state of New York accused D'Agostino of illegitimately taking a state pension. Anyway, the kid says "stained credentials."
<v Ben Calhoun>然后他说了些关于达戈斯蒂诺资历有污点的话。他指的是纽约州指控达戈斯蒂诺非法领取州养老金的事实。总之，这孩子说了“资历有污点”。

00:37:55.810 --> 00:37:57.130
<v Lorenzo Labitigan>--with stained credentials.
<v Lorenzo Labitigan>……资历有污点。

00:37:57.130 --> 00:37:57.330
<v Lorenzo Labitigan>[CLAPPING]
<v Lorenzo Labitigan>[掌声]

00:37:57.330 --> 00:38:00.290
<v Ben Calhoun>D'Agostino jumps in and the crowd erupts.
<v Ben Calhoun>达戈斯蒂诺插话，人群爆发了。

00:38:00.290 --> 00:38:02.620
<v Albert D'agostino>Mr. Chairman, if I might-- if I might--
<v Albert D'agostino>主席先生，如果我能……如果我能……

00:38:02.620 --> 00:38:02.970
<v Albert D'agostino>[CROWD SHOUTING]
<v Albert D'agostino>[人群叫喊]

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:11.300
<v Ben Calhoun>It's hard to hear him in there. D'Agostino's cutting off the student's comment, saying, "Mr. Chairman, if I might-- if I might--"
<v Ben Calhoun>很难听到他在说什么。达戈斯蒂诺打断了学生的评论，说道：“主席先生，如果我能……如果我能……”

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:24.520
<v Albert D'agostino>I take great exception to your term "stained credential." I graduated from an Ivy League college myself, sir, and I don't remember anybody coming up with as ignorant a statement as you just made. You know nothing about me.
<v Albert D'agostino>我对你说的“资历有污点”一词提出强烈反对。我自己也是从常青藤联盟学院毕业的，先生，我不记得任何人能说出像你刚才那样无知的话。你对我一无所知。

00:38:24.520 --> 00:38:25.520
<v Albert D'agostino>[CROWD SHOUTING]
<v Albert D'agostino>[人群叫喊]

00:38:25.520 --> 00:38:27.630
<v Albert D'agostino>If you want to challenge my reputation, do it to me personally.
<v Albert D'agostino>如果你想挑战我的声誉，那就亲自来找我。

00:38:27.630 --> 00:38:29.250
<v Lorenzo Labitigan>Are you threatening me?
<v Lorenzo Labitigan>你在威胁我吗？

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:34.150
<v Ben Calhoun>Right there, the student's asking quizzically, are you threatening me?
<v Ben Calhoun>就在那一刻，学生困惑地问，你在威胁我吗？

00:38:34.150 --> 00:38:42.200
<v Albert D'agostino>No, I'm telling you. I am not a stained lawyer, and I won't take it from anybody, including a freshman Yalie. You're showing your ignorance.
<v Albert D'agostino>不，我在告诉你。我不是一个有污点的律师，我不会从任何人那里接受这种评价，包括一个耶鲁大一新生。你在展示你的无知。

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:46.620
<v Woman>Excuse me, sir. You need to have respect for these children who are graduates--
<v Woman>抱歉，先生。你需要尊重这些毕业生孩子……

00:38:46.620 --> 00:38:54.300
<v Albert  D'agostino>He's not a child. He's an adult. If you make outrageous, defamatory statements, you're going to have to take the response.
<v Albert  D'agostino>他不是孩子。他是成年人。如果你发表离谱的诽谤言论，你必须承担后果。

00:38:54.300 --> 00:39:16.880
<v Ben Calhoun>OK, for the record, as far as these pension allegations go, the New York Comptroller's Office accused D'Agostino of taking a $106,000-per-year New York State pension, one the state says he wasn't eligible for because he wasn't a government employee.
<v Ben Calhoun>好的，记录在案，关于这些养老金指控，纽约州审计长办公室指控达戈斯蒂诺领取每年106,000美元的纽约州养老金，州政府称他不具备领取资格，因为他不是政府雇员。

00:39:16.880 --> 00:39:42.470
<v Ben Calhoun>There's lots of videos of D'Agostino blowing up, yelling at parents, school administrators, pointing fingers in people's faces. There's a well-known incident when D'Agostino verbally abused one of the public school parents. According to a newspaper reporter who saw this, an Honor Roll student came to the parent's defense, and D'Agostino called the kid a piece of shit, asking the child, "Do you want a piece of me?"
<v Ben Calhoun>有很多视频显示达戈斯蒂诺暴跳如雷，对家长、学校行政人员大吼大叫，指着人们的脸。有一个著名的事件是达戈斯蒂诺在言语上虐待了一名公立学校的家长。据目击此事的报纸记者说，一名优等生站出来为那名家长辩护，达戈斯蒂诺称这孩子是“狗屎”，并问那孩子：“你想尝尝我的厉害吗？”

00:39:42.470 --> 00:40:13.970
<v Ben Calhoun>Still, though, the most remarkable example of the bizarro role the school board's law firm has taken on in this conflict, it doesn't actually involve D'Agostino but another attorney from his firm, a lawyer named Chris Kirby. The set-up here is in a board meeting. The lawyer, Kirby, he smirked at a public school parent while she was talking about her special needs kid. When the lawyer, Kirby, eventually left that meeting, an activist named Tony Luciano, he called Kirby an asshole.
<v Ben Calhoun>尽管如此，校董会律师事务所在这场冲突中扮演的角色最令人瞩目的例子，实际上并不涉及达戈斯蒂诺，而是他事务所的另一名律师，名叫克里斯·柯比。背景是一次董事会会议。律师柯比在一名公立学校家长谈论她有特殊需求的孩子时冷笑。当律师柯比最终离开那次会议时，一位名叫托尼·卢西亚诺的活动家称柯比是“混蛋”。

00:40:13.970 --> 00:40:33.760
<v Ben Calhoun>But so Kirby leaves. He's gone. The meeting ends. This group of parents and activists walk out. Luciano, who videotapes every board meeting, is still rolling as they leave. In this video, you see the sidewalk as the parents head to the parking lot. And then a car drives up. I had Luciano, the activist, walk me through what happened.
<v Ben Calhoun>柯比离开了。他走了。会议结束了。那群家长和活动家走了出来。卢西亚诺，那个录下每一次董事会会议的人，在他们离开时还在录像。在这段视频中，你可以看到家长走向停车场的人行道。然后一辆车开了过来。我让活动家卢西亚诺带我重温了发生的事情。

00:40:33.760 --> 00:40:35.460
<v Tony Luciano>A car's just pulling up.
<v Tony Luciano>一辆车正开过来。

00:40:35.460 --> 00:40:36.110
<v Woman>Uh-oh. Uh-oh.
<v Woman>糟了。糟了。

00:40:36.110 --> 00:40:40.890
<v Tony Luciano>I didn't know whose car was pulling up. And he gets out.
<v Tony Luciano>我不知道是谁的车开了过来。他下车了。

00:40:40.890 --> 00:40:42.980
<v Woman>Call the police. Call the police!
<v Woman>报警。报警！

00:40:42.980 --> 00:40:45.840
<v Tony Luciano>Obviously, he laid in wait for us.
<v Tony Luciano>显然他埋伏在那等我们。

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:51.010
<v Ben Calhoun>The lawyer, Kirby, walks right up to the parents. He's the first one you hear in this next clip.
<v Ben Calhoun>律师柯比径直走向家长们。他是你在下一段剪辑中听到的第一个人。

00:40:51.010 --> 00:40:52.370
<v Chris Kirby>You wanna call me an asshole now? Huh?
<v Chris Kirby>你现在想叫我混蛋吗？啊？

00:40:52.370 --> 00:40:53.330
<v Tony Luciano>I already did.
<v Tony Luciano>我已经叫过了。

00:40:53.330 --> 00:40:59.840
<v Chris Kirby>Oh, did you, you fucking cocksucker? Huh? Huh? What? You got a problem?
<v Chris Kirby>哦，你叫了吗，你他妈的混蛋？啊？啊？什么？你有意见？

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:04.600
<v Ben Calhoun>Wait. So can we just pause it right here? Because all you can see is the front of his shirt. What's going on right now?
<v Ben Calhoun>等等。所以我们能在这里停一下吗？因为你只能看到他的衬衫正面。现在发生了什么？

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:10.520
<v Tony Luciano>He's standing directly in front of me.
<v Tony Luciano>他正站在我面前。

00:41:10.520 --> 00:41:16.780
<v Ben Calhoun>And in terms of just how far away from you he is, how much space is between your chest and his chest?
<v Ben Calhoun>至于他离你多远，你的胸部和他的胸部之间有多少空间？

00:41:16.780 --> 00:41:18.430
<v Tony Luciano>None.
<v Tony Luciano>没有。

00:41:18.430 --> 00:41:19.840
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, he's actually up against you?
<v Ben Calhoun>哦，他实际上贴着你？

00:41:19.840 --> 00:41:28.860
<v Tony Luciano>Chest to chest, yeah. Yeah. We're touching. And I noticed that his hands are in a clenched fist, so he's ready to strike me.
<v Tony Luciano>胸贴胸，是的。是的。我们碰在一起了。我注意到他的手紧握成拳，所以他准备打我。

00:41:28.860 --> 00:41:29.500
<v Tony Luciano>I don't got a problem.
<v Tony Luciano>我没意见。

00:41:29.500 --> 00:41:34.230
<v Chris Kirby>Yeah, you do. I'm standing in your way. You gonna do something about it? Yeah, I didn't think so.
<v Chris Kirby>是的，你有。我挡着你的路了。你要对此做点什么吗？是的，我料想你不会。

00:41:34.230 --> 00:41:36.110
<v Tony Luciano>No.
<v Tony Luciano>不。

00:41:36.110 --> 00:41:37.400
<v Woman>Take a picture of his license plate.
<v Woman>拍下他的车牌。

00:41:37.400 --> 00:41:49.460
<v Ben Calhoun>At this point, the lawyer, Kirby, he walks back to his car. Parents are saying, take a picture of his license plate. And Kirby stands next to his car, giving the parents the middle finger. And he keeps saying--
<v Ben Calhoun>此时，律师柯比走回了他的车。家长们说，拍下他的车牌。柯比站在车旁，对着家长们竖中指。他不停地说……

00:41:49.460 --> 00:41:50.190
<v Chris Kirby>Take a picture.
<v Chris Kirby>拍张照。

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:50.910
<v Ben Calhoun>Here you go.
<v Ben Calhoun>给你。

00:41:50.910 --> 00:41:51.990
<v Chris Kirby>Here you go. Take a picture.
<v Chris Kirby>给你。拍张照。

00:41:51.990 --> 00:41:53.120
<v Ben Calhoun>Take a picture. Take a picture.
<v Ben Calhoun>拍张照。拍张照。

00:41:53.120 --> 00:41:54.130
<v Chris Kirby>Take a picture.
<v Chris Kirby>拍张照。

00:41:54.130 --> 00:42:01.080
<v Ben Calhoun>The women get on their phones and they call the cops. And then-- I don't even know. Things just go bananas.
<v Ben Calhoun>女人们拿出手机报警。然后……我甚至不知道。事情变得一团糟。

00:42:01.080 --> 00:42:04.370
<v Peggy Hatton>We're calling the police right now.
<v Peggy Hatton>我们现在就报警。

00:42:04.370 --> 00:42:07.930
