<v Tony Luciano>--school board members have fostered among the attorneys because their disdain for the community has trickled onto the attorneys. They can do anything and get away with it.
<v Tony Luciano>……校董会成员在律师中培养出来的文化，因为他们对社区的蔑视已经渗透到了律师身上。他们可以做任何事而逃脱惩罚。

00:43:15.690 --> 00:43:47.830
<v Ben  Calhoun>After this happened, there was a lot of outcry-- embarrassing coverage, newspaper editorials. The board did say this was too much. It announced publicly it would get rid of D'Agostino's firm. But it would actually take them two years before they'd cut ties with him and the law firm.
<v Ben  Calhoun>这件事发生后，引发了巨大的哗然——尴尬的报道、报纸社论。董事会确实表示这太过分了。它公开宣布将解雇达戈斯蒂诺的事务所。但他们实际上花了两年时间才切断与他和他的律师事务所的联系。

00:43:47.830 --> 00:44:07.310
<v Ben  Calhoun>If I can, I need to change gears here and talk about part of this story that you may have noticed-- that this whole story treads near some of the most destructive anti-Semitic stereotypes in existence. In the last several years, as East Ramapo has divided into us versus them, questions of anti-Semitism have cut a wide path.
<v Ben  Calhoun>如果可以的话，我需要改变一下角度，谈谈这个故事中你可能已经注意到的部分——整个故事触及了现存最具破坏性的反犹太刻板印象。在过去的几年里，当东拉马波分裂为“我们”和“他们”时，关于反犹太主义的问题划出了一条宽阔的界限。

00:44:07.310 --> 00:44:28.770
<v Ben  Calhoun>There have been troubling incidents, cases of kids throwing rocks at school buses full of Hasidic yeshiva students. In one case, a rock hit a young boy and fractured his skull. Last summer, three teenagers drove into Hasidic neighborhoods, shooting at people on the street with a paintball gun. They later told police they'd gone out that night to, quote, "shoot Jews."
<v Ben  Calhoun>发生了令人不安的事件，比如孩子们向装满哈西德派“叶史瓦”学生的校车扔石头。在一次事件中，一块石头击中了一个小男孩并导致其颅骨骨折。去年夏天，三名青少年开车进入哈西德派社区，用彩弹枪射击街上的人。他们后来告诉警察，那天晚上他们出来，引述，“射击犹太人”。

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:45.100
<v Ben  Calhoun>While a lot of activists, parents, and students have tried to keep anyone associated with them from venturing into anti-Semitism, most people have seen glimpses of it. Olivia Castor, the student with the schedules, she told me about organizing rallies to protest the board.
<v Ben  Calhoun>虽然许多活动家、家长和学生试图阻止任何与他们有关的人陷入反犹太主义，但大多数人还是看到了它的踪迹。奥利维亚·卡斯托，那个有课表的学生，告诉我关于组织集会抗议董事会的事情。

00:44:45.100 --> 00:44:51.390
<v Olivia Castor>So when we were doing the rally, people would be like, "F Jews," or something like that. And we were just like, you need to get out.
<v Olivia Castor>所以当我们举行集会时，人们会说“F犹太人”之类的。我们当时就说，你们得离开。

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:55.270
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, so people would actually just try and make that a chant.
<v Ben Calhoun>哦，所以人们实际上只是试图把它变成一句口号。

00:44:55.270 --> 00:45:01.030
<v Olivia Castor>Yeah. You know, they'd say it one time and I'm just like, no. No. I will shut that down right away.
<v Olivia Castor>是的。你知道，他们说了一次，我就说，不。不。我会立即阻止它。

00:45:01.030 --> 00:45:17.480
<v Ben Calhoun>I heard more than one story about students, some very young, going up to teachers and asking, why are the Jews doing this to us? And this dynamic takes a simple political turf war-- a school board fight-- and it turns it into something uglier on all sides.
<v Ben Calhoun>我听到不止一个关于学生的故事，有些非常年轻，走到老师面前问，为什么犹太人要对我们做这种事？这种动态将一场简单的政治地盘争夺战——一场校董会斗争——变成了各方都更加丑陋的事情。

00:45:17.480 --> 00:45:20.160
<v Tony Luciano>They put up their own propaganda videos.
<v Tony Luciano>他们发布了自己的宣传视频。

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:34.330
<v Ben Calhoun>This is Tony Luciano again, the guy who tapes all the meetings, the guy who gets confronted in the parking lot. A few years ago, Luciano ran for school board and someone put up a video against him. I had him show it to me.
<v Ben Calhoun>这是托尼·卢西亚诺，那个录下所有会议的人，那个在停车场被对质的人。几年前，卢西亚诺竞选校董，有人发布了一段针对他的视频。我让他展示给我看。

00:45:34.330 --> 00:45:41.820
<v Tony      Luciano>Let's see. This one.
<v Tony      Luciano>来看看。这一段。

00:45:41.820 --> 00:45:42.420
<v Tony      Luciano>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Tony      Luciano>[音乐播放]

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:46.480
<v Tony      Luciano>It says, "East Ramapo News, Luciano Running for School Board." I didn't put this up.
<v Tony      Luciano>上面写着，“东拉马波新闻，卢西亚诺竞选校董”。这不是我发布的。

00:45:46.480 --> 00:46:12.480
<v Ben Calhoun>The video is essentially an attack ad. A school board election attack ad. In it, there's a clip of Luciano making a comment about Hasidic board members washing their hands of the consequences of their actions, which, for complicated reasons having to do with Pontius Pilate and 2,000 years of Jewish history, this ad says is code for anti-Semitism. And then violin music kicks in.
<v Ben Calhoun>视频本质上是一个攻击性广告。一个校董会选举攻击广告。里面有卢西亚诺的一段评论，说哈西德派董事会成员对自己行为的后果“洗手不管”，由于涉及彼拉多和2000年犹太历史的复杂原因，该广告称这是反犹太主义的暗号。然后小提琴音乐响起。

00:46:12.480 --> 00:46:14.210
<v Man>But to try to absolve--
<v Man>但是为了试图免除……

00:46:14.210 --> 00:46:30.320
<v Tony Luciano>So now they're showing a picture of Nazi soldiers high-stepping. "Vote no to bigotry and hatred." And again, they show the Germans, Nazi soldiers, high-stepping.
<v Tony Luciano>所以现在他们展示了一张纳粹士兵高抬腿行进的照片。“对偏见和仇恨投反对票”。而且他们再次展示了德国纳粹士兵高抬腿的照片。

00:46:30.320 --> 00:46:34.090
<v Ben Calhoun>Did you say that there was one that was harsher and more offensive than this one?
<v Ben Calhoun>你说过有一个比这个更严厉、更具冒犯性的视频？

00:46:34.090 --> 00:46:35.820
<v Tony Luciano>Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.
<v Tony Luciano>哦，是的，哦，是的。

00:46:35.820 --> 00:46:37.410
<v Ben Calhoun>How did the other one go?
<v Ben Calhoun>另一个是怎么样的？

00:46:37.410 --> 00:46:47.270
<v Tony Luciano>It showed Adolph Hitler talking. It showed people in the concentration camps, in Auschwitz. It showed--
<v Tony Luciano>它展示了阿道夫·希特勒在说话。它展示了奥斯威辛集中营里的人们。它展示了……

00:46:47.270 --> 00:47:03.740
<v Ben Calhoun>One of the other public school activists, Peggy Hatton, the mom who gets cursed out in the parking lot, a while ago someone took a picture of her, put a mustache on it, split-screened it with a picture of Hitler, and that got tweeted through the Hasidic and Orthodox community.
<v Ben Calhoun>另一位公立学校活动家，佩吉·哈顿，那个在停车场被咒骂的母亲，不久前有人拍了她的照片，贴上一撇小胡子，与希特勒的照片做成对比图，并在哈西德派和正统派社区中推特传播。

00:47:03.740 --> 00:47:22.480
<v Ben Calhoun>The tension of all of this-- real anti-Semitism, accusations of anti-Semitism, the fear of anti-Semitism, and the fear of being accused of anti-Semitism-- it casts a fog over this whole situation. There's an incident from a school board meeting. It's kind of well-known by people here.
<v Ben Calhoun>这一切的紧张关系——真正的反犹太主义、对反犹太主义的指控、对反犹太主义的恐惧，以及因被指控反犹太主义而产生的恐惧——给整个局面笼罩上了一层迷雾。有一个来自董事会会议的事件。当地人对此非常熟悉。

00:47:22.480 --> 00:47:37.780
<v Ben Calhoun>It was the board president at the time, Daniel Schwartz, who was leading the Hasidic majority in 2012. And he gave this eight-minute speech at a board meeting. Public school activists have nicknamed it Schwartz's Rant. He starts out by bringing up a few different troubling incidents.
<v Ben Calhoun>那是当时的董事会主席丹尼尔·施瓦茨，他在2012年领导哈西德派多数派。他在一次董事会会议上发表了八分钟的演讲。公立学校活动家将其昵称为“施瓦茨的咆哮”。他首先提到了一些令人不安的事件。

00:47:37.780 --> 00:47:55.980
<v Daniel  Schwartz>The children in our schools are walking up to their teachers and, apparently in response to the demographic makeup of this board, have been telling their teachers, many of whom are of the Jewish faith, that they hate Jews.
<v Daniel  Schwartz>我们学校的孩子们走到老师面前，显然是对董事会人口结构的回应，告诉他们的老师（其中许多是犹太信仰的人）他们恨犹太人。

00:47:55.980 --> 00:48:14.430
<v Ben Calhoun>The thing that Schwartz does after this, where things get super complicated, he starts to cast a blanket accusation of anti-Semitism over all the people who had been opposing the school board's decisions. He starts by bringing up a student who'd come to a meeting to challenge the board.
<v Ben Calhoun>施瓦茨在此之后所做的事情让情况变得极其复杂，他开始将反犹太主义的笼统指控抛向所有反对董事会决定的人。他首先提到了一个前来会议挑战董事会的学生。

00:48:14.430 --> 00:48:40.530
<v Daniel Schwartz>During another controversial period this past year, a young student stood up at that rostrum there and stated that, as far as she is concerned, this board cares more about money and taxes than it does about people. That too raises an age-old anti-Semitic trope of Jewish obsession with money.
<v Daniel Schwartz>在去年另一个争议时期，一名年轻学生站在讲台上说，就她而言，这个董事会关心金钱和税收胜过关心人。这也引发了犹太人对金钱痴迷的古老反犹太主义寓意。

00:48:40.530 --> 00:49:20.500
<v Daniel Schwartz>I simply won't have it. I will not tolerate-- I will not have any such statements ever being made in this district. Suggesting that Orthodox Jews who do not send their children to the public school lack the moral authority to hold this office hearkens back to the admonishments of Saint Augustine, who famously stated that the Jews should be allowed to live but never be allowed to prosper. And that paved the road to Auschwitz and the crematory of Treblinka.
<v Daniel Schwartz>我简直无法接受。我绝不会容忍——我绝不会允许本学区再出现任何此类陈述。暗示那些不把孩子送到公立学校的正统派犹太人缺乏担任此职的道德权威，这让人想起圣奥古斯丁的告诫，他曾著名地声称应该允许犹太人活着，但绝不能允许他们繁荣。而这为通向奥斯威辛和特雷布林卡火葬场铺平了道路。

00:49:20.500 --> 00:49:34.180
<v Daniel Schwartz>To suggest that we lack the moral authority to sit in these seats, let me tell you right now-- you don't like it, find yourself another place to live because this is the United States of America.
<v Daniel Schwartz>要说我们缺乏担任这些席位的道德权威，让我现在就告诉你们——你们不喜欢它，就找个别的地方住吧，因为这是美利坚合众国。

00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:35.690
<v Woman>Yes, it is.
<v Woman>是的，它是。

00:49:35.690 --> 00:49:38.920
<v Daniel   Schwartz>Not only that--
<v Daniel   Schwartz>不仅如此……

00:49:38.920 --> 00:49:39.790
<v Daniel   Schwartz>[ONE PERSON APPLAUDING]
<v Daniel   Schwartz>[一个人鼓掌]

00:49:39.790 --> 00:50:18.680
<v Ben Calhoun>To complicate all this even more, consider the racial dynamics of this situation. On one side, the Hasidim, all white. On the other side, the public school families, almost entirely black and Latino. But in school board meetings, in rallies, campaign literature, interestingly, racism doesn't come up the way anti-Semitism does. The activists told me they try to leave it off the table because they know how explosive it is. But in private conversations, it comes up.
<v Ben Calhoun>为了让这一切更加复杂，考虑一下这种情况下的种族动态。一方面是哈西德派，全白人。另一方面是公立学校家庭，几乎全是黑人和拉丁裔。但在校董会会议、集会、竞选宣传品中，有趣的是，种族主义并没有像反犹太主义那样被提及。活动家告诉我，他们尽量避而不谈，因为他们知道这有多么爆炸性。但在私下交谈中，它还是会被提及。

00:50:18.680 --> 00:50:55.740
<v Ben Calhoun>I spent months talking to people on both sides of this situation, and I think whatever role anti-Semitism has in all of this, the bigger split is the chasm between an isolationist religious community and everyone else. I think secular people sense from these religious groups an implied disdain and disapproval for their way of life. And in return, secular people feel a disdain and disapproval back towards the religious community over lots of issues, including things like how these groups often treat women. This animosity, it's ambient, and it's hard to pin down. But it's corrosive.
<v Ben Calhoun>我花了几个月时间与双方的人交谈，我认为无论反犹太主义在其中扮演了什么角色，更大的鸿沟是孤立主义宗教社区与其他人之间的鸿沟。我认为世俗的人从这些宗教团体中感知到一种对他们的生活方式隐含的蔑视和不赞同。作为回报，世俗的人感到一种反向的蔑视和不赞同，涉及许多问题，包括这些群体通常如何对待女性。这种敌意是氛围性的，很难确定，但它具有腐蚀性。

00:50:55.740 --> 00:51:05.190
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>I personally don't see a whole lot of anti-Semitism.
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>我个人并没有看到太多的反犹太主义。

00:51:05.190 --> 00:51:20.240
<v Ben Calhoun>Suzanne Young-Mercer deals with all kinds of people in her job running the emergency room at Mount Sinai, one of New York's largest hospitals. Again, she served on the school board. And she told me this anecdote that encapsulates a lot of what I saw in Ramapo.
<v Ben Calhoun>苏珊·杨-默瑟在运营纽约最大的医院之一——西奈山医院的急诊室时，与各种各样的人打交道。同样，她曾在校董会任职。她告诉我这段轶事，概括了我在拉马波所看到的很多东西。

00:51:20.240 --> 00:51:42.840
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>One day, I was walking down the street. And there was a Hasidic woman who called me. She said, miss? Miss? And I looked at her. And she said, I wonder if you could help me. She's in her home. So I walk over and I say, what do you need me to do?
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>有一天，我走在街上。有一位哈西德派女士叫我。她说，小姐？小姐？我看着她。她说，我想请你帮个忙。她在家里。所以我走过去说，你需要我做什么？

00:51:42.840 --> 00:51:52.780
<v Ben Calhoun>Turned out, it was Friday-- Sabbath-- and the woman had a switch that she wasn't allowed to flip on her fridge. Young-Mercer went in and flipped it for her.
<v Ben Calhoun>结果那天是周五——安息日——那位女士冰箱上有一个开关，她不能动。杨-默瑟进去帮她关上了。

00:51:52.780 --> 00:52:10.460
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>And she took out these little fruits or something. And she said, oh, thank you so much. Here, take this. They're really very good. You can take these. And so I took them. She said, thank you so much. And I left the house.
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>她拿出这些小水果之类的东西。她说，哦，太感谢你了。拿着这个。它们非常好吃。你可以拿着。所以我拿着了。她说，太感谢你了。我就离开了那个房子。

00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:48.990
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>I don't understand why a little tiny switch on her refrigerator couldn't be pushed down by them without God sending his wrath down. I don't understand that whole part of it. But I was willing to help them because why not? You're my neighbor. You asked me for some help. As little as that was to do, certainly it's something I could do. I know truly in my heart that if I needed help, if I was standing on a street corner and I needed help from one of them, they would walk over me before they'd help me. And that's the difference between them and us.
<v Suzanne Young-mercer>我不明白为什么冰箱上一个小小的开关，在上帝不降下愤怒之前，他们就不能自己按下。我不明白这整件事。但我愿意帮助他们，因为为什么不呢？你是我的邻居。你请求我帮忙。尽管这件事微不足道，但这当然是我能做到的。我心中确实知道，如果我需要帮助，如果我站在街角需要他们其中一个人的帮助，他们会在帮我之前就从我身上跨过去。这就是他们和我们之间的区别。

00:52:48.990 --> 00:53:18.180
<v Ben   Calhoun>I don't think Suzanne Young-Mercer is anti-Semitic. But I do think anytime you say, I know in my heart what everyone from this one particular group thinks and feels, that's trouble. And in East Ramapo, that trouble is all around you.
<v Ben   Calhoun>我不认为苏珊·杨-默瑟是反犹太主义者。但我确实认为，每当你断言“我知道这个特定群体中的每个人是怎么想和感受的”时，那就是麻烦。而在东拉马波，这种麻烦无处不在。

00:53:18.180 --> 00:53:30.510
<v Ben   Calhoun>There are enough Hasidim to control the local school board in East Ramapo, but there aren't enough to control New York State. And increasingly, the board is not just at war with the parents and their district, but with state government.
<v Ben   Calhoun>哈西德派的人数足以控制东拉马波的当地校董会，但不足以控制纽约州。董事会不仅在与家长和他们的学区交战，而且越来越多地与州政府交战。

00:53:30.510 --> 00:53:59.570
<v Ben   Calhoun>In 2010 and again in 2012, the New York State Education Department reviewed what the district was doing-- placing Hasidic special ed students in private schools using public money. Both times, the state said, stop what you're doing. It's against both federal and state law. We'll cut your funding. This changed nothing. The district did not comply. What it did was sue the state. The court said the state was right, but the board is appealing.
<v Ben   Calhoun>2010年和2012年，纽约州教育局审查了学区的行为——用公款将哈西德派特教学生安置在私立学校。两次，州政府都说，停止你们的行为。这违反了联邦法和州法。我们会削减你们的经费。这没有改变任何事。学区没有遵守。它所做的是起诉州政府。法院判决州政府正确，但董事会上诉了。

00:53:59.570 --> 00:54:19.510
<v Ben   Calhoun>In 2011, the New York State Comptroller conducted an audit of the district's finances. The audit determined that the board has engaged in inaccurate record-keeping with discrepancies in the millions of dollars, loose monitoring of money going to private schools, sloppy bill payment, and just all kinds of bad management.
<v Ben   Calhoun>2011年，纽约州审计长对学区的财务进行了审计。审计认定，董事会从事了不准确的记录保存，存在数百万美元的差异、对流向私立学校的资金监控松散、账单支付草率，以及各种糟糕的管理。

00:54:19.510 --> 00:55:00.810
<v Ben   Calhoun>The other big category here is the district's real estate deals. The board did close two schools and sold them to yeshivas. One of those deals, in particular, it's been a complete mess. In short, the district got a bid from a yeshiva for the building. The bid was for $3 million. The school building was appraised at $6 million. So that's a hard deal to justify-- until the board rushed out and got the school reappraised for $3 million and sold it to the yeshiva at that price. Activists challenged the new appraisal, and the state took their side and nixed the deal. The appraiser recently pleaded guilty to fraud charges.
<v Ben   Calhoun>另一个大类别是学区的房地产交易。董事会确实关闭了两所学校并将它们卖给了“叶史瓦”学校。其中一项交易特别乱。简而言之，学区收到了“叶史瓦”学校对该建筑的报价。报价是300万美元。该校舍估价为600万美元。所以这是一项难以辩护的交易——直到董事会急忙找人将该学校重新估价为300万美元，并以该价格卖给了“叶史瓦”学校。活动家对新的评估提出了质疑，州政府站在他们一边并取消了交易。评估师最近承认了欺诈指控。

00:55:00.810 --> 00:55:18.900
<v Ben   Calhoun>Just this summer, New York Governor Andrew Cuomo appointed a monitor to watch the district. The district's response? The current board president wrote a letter saying the state was, quote, "acceding to the demands of bigots and lending officials sanction to their prejudice," end quote.
<v Ben   Calhoun>就在今年夏天，纽约州长科莫任命了一名监督员来监视该学区。学区的回应呢？现任董事会主席写了一封信，称州政府是“屈从于偏执狂的要求，并给予官员对其偏见的制裁”。

00:55:18.900 --> 00:55:25.650
<v Steve White>I've never had to write anybody in before. So I'm voting for Governor Cuomo, OK?
<v Steve White>我以前从没不得不写谁的名字。所以我投了科莫州长的票，好吗？

00:55:25.650 --> 00:55:53.280
<v Ben Calhoun>This past May, I met up with public school activist Steve White. This was school board election day. I stood in the booth with him as he wrote in Governor Cuomo three times for the East Ramapo school board. He had to do that because, for the first time since all this started, there were no public school candidates running. After years of not winning a single race, the public school crowd failed to recruit anybody to run.
<v Ben Calhoun>去年五月，我见到了公立学校活动家史蒂夫·怀特。那是校董会选举日。我站在投票箱前，看他三次写下科莫州长的名字参加东拉马波校董会竞选。他不得不这样做，因为自这一切开始以来第一次，没有公立学校候选人参选。在多年没有赢得一场竞选后，公立学校阵营没能招募到任何人参加竞选。

00:55:53.280 --> 00:56:06.330
<v Ben Calhoun>During much of this fight, Steve was kind of the field general for the public school side. We stood outside his polling place, where, in recent years, he says there were huge lines. But today, there was no one. We were the only two people on the sidewalk.
<v Ben Calhoun>在这场斗争的大部分时间里，史蒂夫算是公立学校阵营的前线指挥官。我们站在他的投票站外，他说近年来那里有排长队的情况。但今天，一个人都没有。我们是人行道上唯一的两个人。

00:56:06.330 --> 00:56:23.820
<v Steve White>If someone wanted to organize this year, they could very well have gotten their candidates together, gotten their signatures signed, handed them in, shown up here today to do battle with your cards in hand and as people arrive to vote. It's just that no one did.
<v Steve White>如果有人想在今年组织起来，他们完全可以召集候选人、签好名字、递交上去，今天出现在这里，带着他们的卡片，在人们到达投票时进行战斗。只是没人这样做。

00:56:23.820 --> 00:56:28.490
<v Ben  Calhoun>Does that feel like giving up at all?
<v Ben  Calhoun>这感觉像是放弃了吗？

00:56:28.490 --> 00:56:33.260
<v Steve White>I guess it does. Yeah, I guess it does.
<v Steve White>我想是的。是的，我想是的。

00:56:33.260 --> 00:56:52.210
<v Ben Calhoun>White says, these days, even if his fellow public school activists organize better than ever, he doesn't think they could win a seat. The Hasidic community is now just too big. He estimates they're half of registered voters now. That door is closed, he told me.
<v Ben Calhoun>怀特说，如今，即使他的公立学校活动家同伴比以往任何时候都组织得更好，他也不认为他们能赢得席位。哈西德社区现在太大了。他估计他们现在占注册选民的一半。那扇门关上了，他告诉我。

00:56:52.210 --> 00:57:08.140
<v Ben Calhoun>Eventually, he stopped the car at Hillcrest Elementary School. This is the school that was appraised for $6 million and sold for $3 million to a yeshiva. The state stepped in and voided the sale, but it eventually went through this summer for $4.9 million.
<v Ben Calhoun>最后，他把车停在希尔克雷斯特小学。这就是那所被估价600万美元并以300万美元卖给“叶史瓦”学校的学校。州政府介入并作废了交易，但在今年夏天，它最终以490万美元的价格成交。

00:57:08.140 --> 00:57:22.330
<v Ben Calhoun>We walked around the back of the building, and you could see why the school was so sought after. Directly behind it sits one of the largest Hasidic communities in the area. The only thing separating the two was a field and a small chain-link fence.
<v Ben Calhoun>我们在建筑后面走了一圈，你就能明白为什么学校如此抢手了。在它后面直接就是该地区最大的哈西德社区之一。分隔两者的只有一片田地和一个小铁丝网围栏。

00:57:22.330 --> 00:57:32.170
<v Steve White>Yeah, so there's the way you can walk through. We don't have to go any further. You see those buildings there through the fence? Yeah. And see where people are walking through? That's where they broke a hole in the fence.
<v Steve White>是的，所以你可以从这里走过去。我们不用再往后走了。你看到围栏那边的那些建筑了吗？是的。看到人们走过的地方了吗？那就是他们把围栏弄破的地方。

00:57:32.170 --> 00:57:57.960
<v Ben Calhoun>As we stood there, a steady stream of Hasidic families made their way through a hole in the fence and towards the school building. Though, today, they weren't there for school, for the yeshiva. This was election day, and this yeshiva, it doubles as a polling place. The Hasidic families walking towards the building, they were all on their way to cast their votes.
<v Ben Calhoun>当我们站在那里时，源源不断的哈西德派家庭通过围栏上的洞向学校建筑走去。虽然今天他们不是为了上学，为了“叶史瓦”学校而来。这是选举日，而这个“叶史瓦”学校，同时也是投票站。哈西德派家庭向建筑走去，他们都在去投票的路上。

00:57:57.960 --> 00:58:25.860
<v Ira  Glass>Ben Calhoun is one of the producers of our show. So right after this program first aired back in 2014, the fiscal monitor from the state wrote up a report about East Ramapo, calling it, quote, "a school district in crisis." Since then, the state legislator has continued keeping an eye on the district, appointing new monitors, and passing a bill that requires the state education commissioner to approve the local East Ramapo school board budget.
<v Ira  Glass>本·卡尔霍恩是我们节目的制片人之一。所以就在这个节目于2014年首次播出后，来自州政府的财政监督员写了一份关于东拉马波的报告，称其为“陷入危机的学区”。从那时起，州议员继续密切关注该学区，任命了新的监督员，并投票通过了一项要求州教育专员必须批准东拉马波当地校董会预算的法案。

00:58:25.860 --> 00:58:38.130
<v Ira  Glass>In the pension case against Al D'Agostino-- thats the one that accused him of getting a state pension while not a state employee, the case that led the student to say that he had stained credentials-- the state ended up ruling in favor of D'Agostino.
<v Ira  Glass>在针对阿尔·达戈斯蒂诺的养老金案件中——就是那个指控他在不是州雇员的情况下领取州养老金的案件，那个导致学生说他资历有污点的案件——州政府最终裁定达戈斯蒂诺胜诉。

00:58:38.130 --> 00:59:09.360
<v Ira  Glass>In the 2015 school board elections, there were pro-public school candidates on the ballot again. Today, one of them is on the board. This past school year, the school board accepted funds from the state to restore some of the things that have been cut. All-day kindergarten was brought back, along with some music and art classes. Public school advocates say that things are slowly getting better. Just this week, the community voted on and passed a budget that increases summer programs, arts, bilingual classes, and special education.
<v Ira  Glass>在2015年的校董会选举中，支持公立学校的候选人再次出现在选票上。今天，其中一人就在董事会中。过去一个学年，校董会接受了来自州政府的资金，以恢复一些被削减的项目。全日制幼儿园恢复了，音乐和艺术课程也恢复了一些。公立学校倡导者说情况正在慢慢变好。就在本周，社区投票通过了一项增加暑期项目、艺术、双语课程和特殊教育的预算。

00:59:09.360 --> 01:00:15.270
<v Ira  Glass>[MUSIC - "DON'T THROW MY VOTE AWAY" BY DAVE STEIN]
<v Ira  Glass>[音乐 - "DON'T THROW MY VOTE AWAY" BY DAVE STEIN]

01:00:15.270 --> 01:00:19.150
<v Woman>I can't believe I'm even hearing this! That is disgusting!
<v Woman>我简直不敢相信我在听这些！那太恶心了！

01:00:19.150 --> 01:00:24.150
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《这就是美国生活》的故事。
